// ══ UGC NET Paper 1 — Unit IX — People, Development & Environment — Previous Year Questions ══
// Paper 1 bank: goes into PAPER1_QB (kept separate from Paper 2 MASTER_QB).
// Format: {unit,paper,no,q,opts,ans,exp}  (+ alt:[extra accepted option idx], bonus:1 = dropped by NTA, full marks to all)
// paper = PYQ paper key (see PAPER1_PAPERS in the website); no = question order inside that paper.

PAPER1_QB.push(

  // ── June 2026 · Set 1 ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun26_a',no:14,q:'Senior citizens who continue learning will ____________.',opts:['A) Never suffer from Alzheimer and senile dementia','B) Suffer from Alzheimer and senile dementia like all old persons','C) Reduce chances of suffering from Alzheimer and senile dementia','D) Suffer from disabilities other than Alzheimer\'s and senile dementia'],ans:2,exp:'Continued learning and mental engagement are associated with reduced (not eliminated) risk of cognitive decline in old age – a moderate, evidence-based claim, unlike the absolute claims in options (A) and (B). Why the others fail: (A) and (B) make absolute claims (\'never suffer\' / \'suffer like all old persons\') that are too strong to be evidence-based. (D) introduces disabilities unrelated to cognitive decline. Only (C)\'s moderate, evidence-based \'reduce chances of\' framing matches how continued learning actually affects cognitive-decline risk. Exam tip: In People/Development/Environment questions about health claims, options using absolute words (\'never\', \'always\', \'all\') are usually distractors — correct options tend to use moderate, hedged language (\'reduce\', \'may\', \'tend to\').'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun26_a',no:15,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II. A. Skill India Mission B. Pradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY) C. Skill Hub D. Micro-Credentials. I. Nodal Skill Centres II. Skill based Courses and Training Programme III. Skill Ecosystem IV. Skill Development and Vocational Training opportunities',opts:['A) A-II,B-IV,C-I,D-III','B) A-I,B-IV,C-II,D-III','C) A-III,B-IV,C-II,D-I','D) A-III,B-IV,C-I,D-II'],ans:3,exp:'Skill India Mission is the overarching skill ecosystem (III). PMKVY provides vocational training opportunities to youth (IV). Skill Hubs act as nodal skill centres (I). Micro-credentials are short, skill-based courses/training programmes (II). Why the others fail: (A), (B) and (C) each misassign at least one scheme — commonly swapping Skill India Mission\'s \'ecosystem\' framing (III) with PMKVY\'s \'training opportunities\' framing (IV), since both schemes sound similar at a surface level. Matching each name to its defining role (umbrella mission vs. specific scheme vs. centre vs. credential) resolves the ambiguity. Exam tip: Treat Skill India Mission as the umbrella \'ecosystem\', with PMKVY, Skill Hubs, and Micro-Credentials as its component parts — visualising the hierarchy (mission $\\to$ scheme $\\to$ centre $\\to$ credential) makes these matching questions much easier.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun26_a',no:28,q:'Which of the following are true about Modified Mercalli Scale (MMS)? A. It measures the impact upon people. B. It is also known as hazard manager\'s scale. C. Scale size in MMS is much smaller than Richter Scale. D. Uses linear scale for the measurement.',opts:['A) A, B and C only','B) B, C and D only','C) A, B and D only','D) A, C and D only'],ans:2,exp:'MMS assesses earthquake intensity by its perceived impact on people and structures (A), and unlike the logarithmic Richter scale, MMS is a linear intensity scale (D). It is used practically for on-the-ground hazard/damage assessment purposes (B). Why the others fail: (A), (B) and (D) each wrongly include C — MMS is actually a much larger-numbered scale (I to XII) than the Richter scale\'s typical single-digit range, not smaller, so C is factually false and any option retaining it is incorrect. Exam tip: Keep the Richter-vs-Mercalli contrast clear: Richter = logarithmic, measures magnitude (energy released); Modified Mercalli = linear, measures intensity (perceived impact), scaled I–XII — a classic Environment-unit pairing question.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun26_a',no:33,q:'Which of the following is a biochemical treatment process in the context of waste water?',opts:['A) Activated Sludge','B) Sedimentation','C) Filtration','D) Grit Chamber'],ans:0,exp:'Activated sludge treatment uses micro-organisms to biologically/biochemically break down organic matter in wastewater. Sedimentation, filtration, and grit chambers are all purely physical treatment processes. Why the others fail: (B) Sedimentation, (C) Filtration and (D) Grit Chamber are all purely physical treatment steps (settling, straining, removing grit by gravity) with no biological/chemical action involved, unlike activated sludge, which relies on micro-organisms. Exam tip: Wastewater-treatment questions often test the physical/chemical/biological classification of individual steps — activated sludge, trickling filters, and anaerobic digestion are biological; sedimentation, screening, and filtration are physical.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun26_a',no:35,q:'Blue baby syndrome, a water borne disease in human infants, is known as',opts:['A) Oxyhemoglobinemia','B) Memoglobinemia','C) Metaglobinemia','D) Methemoglobinemia'],ans:3,exp:'Methemoglobinemia ("blue baby syndrome") is caused chiefly by nitrate-contaminated drinking water, which oxidises haemoglobin to methemoglobin, impairing oxygen transport and giving infants a bluish skin tone. Why the others fail: (A), (B) and (C) are not recognised medical terms — they are near-miss spellings of the correct term. Only \'Methemoglobinemia\' is the accepted clinical name for blue baby syndrome. Exam tip: Environment-unit questions sometimes test recognition of a correctly spelled technical term among plausible near-miss distractors — read each option letter-by-letter rather than pattern-matching on the general \'looks right\' shape of the word.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun26_a',no:37,q:'Which of the following are true about Thermal Pollution? A. It is a kind of water pollution. B. This can be caused from thermal power plants. C. It can severely affect the aquatic life. D. It increases the Dissolved Oxygen (DO) of water body.',opts:['A) A, B, C and D','B) B, C and D only','C) A, B and C only','D) A and D only'],ans:2,exp:'Thermal pollution is indeed a form of water pollution (A), commonly caused by heated discharge from thermal power plants (B), and it harms aquatic life by disrupting temperature-sensitive ecosystems (C). However, it decreases – not increases – dissolved oxygen, since warmer water holds less dissolved gas, so D is false. Why the others fail: (A), (B) and (D) all wrongly retain D — thermal pollution actually decreases dissolved oxygen (warmer water holds less dissolved gas), the opposite of what D claims, so any option keeping D is factually incorrect. Exam tip: A useful chemistry fact behind this question: gas solubility in water falls as temperature rises — this single principle explains both why thermal pollution lowers DO and why warm water bodies are more prone to fish kills.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun26_a',no:42,q:'What was the concentration (Atmospheric level) of carbon dioxide (CO$_2$) just before the industrial revolution?',opts:['A) 250 ppmv','B) 280 ppmv','C) 300 ppmv','D) 320 ppmv'],ans:1,exp:'The commonly cited pre-industrial atmospheric CO$_2$ concentration is approximately 280 ppm, compared with over 400 ppm today. Why the others fail: (A) 250 ppmv understates and (C) 300 / (D) 320 ppmv overstate the historically cited pre-industrial baseline; 280 ppm is the standard reference figure used across climate-science literature and environment-syllabus content. Exam tip: Keep two CO$_2$ benchmark numbers together for revision: $\\sim$280 ppm pre-industrial baseline vs. 400+ ppm today — the gap itself is often the point being tested, not just the raw pre-industrial figure.'},
  // ── 06 Jan 2026 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_c',no:36,q:'Which one among the following gases has the highest Global Warming Potential (GWP)?',opts:['A) Sulphur dioxide (SO$_2$)','B) Nitrous Oxide (N$_2$O)','C) Methane (CH$_4$)','D) Carbon dioxide (CO$_2$)'],ans:1,exp:'Among the four listed gases, Nitrous Oxide has a dramatically higher heat-trapping capacity per unit mass than both Methane and Carbon dioxide, and Sulphur dioxide is not conventionally ranked among significant greenhouse-warming gases at all – confirming N$_2$O as the correct answer, Option (B).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_c',no:37,q:'Identify the waste disposal method which is used for burning hospital waste at high temperature ($>670^{\\circ}$C) in the presence of air in a properly constructed furnace.',opts:['A) Compositing','B) Incineration','C) Pyrolysis','D) Pulverization'],ans:1,exp:'The defining, distinguishing phrase in the question is “in the presence of air” – this single detail rules out Pyrolysis (which specifically requires the absence of oxygen) and confirms Incineration, the standard high-temperature, oxygen-present combustion method used for hospital/biomedical waste disposal in India under the Bio-Medical Waste Management Rules.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_c',no:38,q:'Atmospheric concentrations of which of the following gases are used for the calculation of Air Quality Index (AQI) in the urban regions? A. Carbon monoxide ; B. Carbon dioxide ; C. Nitrogen dioxide ; D. Sulphur dioxide ; E. Argon Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B and C Only','B) A, D and E Only','C) A, C, D and E Only','D) A, C and D Only'],ans:3,exp:'The CPCB\'s AQI is explicitly built from a defined list of eight criteria pollutants; among the five gases offered, exactly three (CO, NO$_2$, SO$_2$) appear on that list, while CO$_2$ and Argon do not (CO$_2$ belongs to greenhouse-gas/climate monitoring, and Argon is chemically inert with no health/air-quality significance) – giving Option (D).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_c',no:39,q:'Arrange the following in the increasing order of their degree of Coalification? A. Bituminous ; B. Anthracite ; C. Sub-bituminous ; D. Peat ; E. Lignite Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) D, E, C, A, B','B) D, C, A, B, E','C) D, E, A, C, B','D) E, D, A, C, B'],ans:0,exp:'Coalification is a linear, well-defined geological progression driven by increasing temperature, pressure, and burial time, transforming low-carbon peat into high-carbon anthracite through the intermediate stages of lignite, sub-bituminous, and bituminous coal. Reciting this standard mnemonic sequence directly gives D, E, C, A, B – Option (A).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_c',no:40,q:'Match List – I with List – II. [2@lList – I (Act): A. Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act ; B. The National Green Tribunal Act ; C. The Wildlife Protection Act, ; D. The Forest (Conservation) Act]<br>[List – II (Year): I. 1980 ; II. 1981 ; III. 2010 ; IV. 1972] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','B) A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II','C) A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','D) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I'],ans:3,exp:'This is a direct recall question testing the enactment years of four landmark Indian environmental statutes. Matching each Act to its well-documented year of enactment (Wildlife Protection Act, 1972; Forest Conservation Act, 1980; Air Act, 1981; NGT Act, 2010) gives the mapping A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I, confirming Option (D).'},
  // ── 05 Jan 2026 · Morning Shift 1 ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_a',no:36,q:'Which of the following is not a gaseous pollutant in the atmosphere?',opts:['A) Tropospheric ozone','B) Sulphur dioxide','C) Atmosphere aerosols','D) Nitrogen dioxide'],ans:2,exp:'Ozone, sulphur dioxide, and nitrogen dioxide are all true gaseous molecules and are classified as gaseous pollutants. Aerosols, by contrast, are suspensions of tiny solid or liquid particles in the air (particulate matter, PM), which is a fundamentally different physical state from a gas – making Option C the correct answer.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_a',no:37,q:'In Earth\'s geological history, which one of the following period is characterized by large-scale formation of coal?',opts:['A) Cambrian period','B) Ordovician period','C) Silurian period','D) Carboniferous period'],ans:3,exp:'The very name “Carboniferous” derives from “carbon-bearing,” directly referencing the massive coal seams laid down during this period from decayed swamp-forest vegetation. None of the other three periods (Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian) are associated with large-scale coal deposition, making Option D the clear answer.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_a',no:38,q:'Arrange the following International agreements/efforts pertaining to environmental protection in chronological order. A. Stockholm conference (UNCHE) ; B. Paris agreement ; C. Montreal protocol ; D. Kyoto protocol ; E. Earth Summit (UNCED) Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, E, C, D, B','B) A, C, E, D, B','C) C, E, A, B, D','D) E, A, C, B, D'],ans:1,exp:'Arranging strictly by year of occurrence – Stockholm (1972), Montreal Protocol (1987), Earth Summit (1992), Kyoto Protocol (1997), Paris Agreement (2015) – yields the sequence A, C, E, D, B, exactly Option B. A common trap is assuming the Earth Summit (Rio, 1992) came before the Montreal Protocol (1987), but the Montreal Protocol is in fact five years earlier.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_a',no:39,q:'India\'s ethanol blending programme aims to achieve which of the following key objectives? A. to promote India\'s energy security. ; B. to achieve reduction in Greenhouse gas emissions. ; C. to promote ethanol imports at large-scale. ; D. to strengthen rural economy. ; E. to facilitate waste-to-wealth conversion. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B and C Only','B) B, C and D Only','C) C, D and E Only','D) A, B, D and E Only'],ans:3,exp:'Four of the five listed objectives – energy security, emissions reduction, rural economic strengthening, and waste-to-wealth conversion – are genuine, documented goals of India\'s Ethanol Blending Programme. Statement C is the outlier: the programme\'s entire premise is to reduce reliance on imports (both of crude oil and, by extension, of ethanol itself) by boosting domestic production, making C false.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_a',no:40,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II [LIST-I (Environment related Acts): A. Forest Conservation Act ; B. Wildlife (Protection) Act ; C. Environment (Protection) Act ; D. Water (Prevention and control of Pollution) Act]<br>[LIST-II (Year): I. 1974 ; II. 1980 ; III. 1972 ; IV. 1986] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','B) A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II','C) A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III','D) A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I'],ans:0,exp:'Each Act is matched to its historically documented enactment year: Wildlife Protection Act (1972, the earliest of the four), Water Act (1974), Forest Conservation Act (1980), and Environment (Protection) Act (1986, the latest, enacted in the aftermath of the Bhopal Gas Tragedy). This produces the mapping A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I, exactly Option A.'},
  // ── 02 Jan 2026 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_b',no:36,q:'Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R [4pt]Assertion A: Eutrophication leads to depletion of dissolved oxygen in a lake. [4pt]Reason R: Dissolved oxygen in water decreases with increase in temperature. [6pt]In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below',opts:['A) Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A','B) Both A and R are correct but R is NOT the correct explanation of A','C) A is correct but R is not correct','D) A is not correct but R is correct'],ans:1,exp:'This question tests whether a candidate can distinguish between two independently true statements that are topically related (both concern dissolved oxygen in water) but are not causally linked in the way the question implies. Eutrophication\'s oxygen depletion mechanism is biological (decomposer bacteria consuming oxygen while breaking down excess algal biomass), entirely distinct from the physical/thermodynamic fact that oxygen\'s solubility in water decreases as temperature rises.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_b',no:37,q:'Arrange the following in ascending order of their moisture content. A. Trash ; B. Rubbish ; C. Refuse ; D. Garbage Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C, D','B) B, A, C, D','C) A, B, D, C','D) D, C, B, A'],ans:0,exp:'In solid waste management terminology, these four terms are not interchangeable synonyms (as in everyday English) but represent a precise gradient based on moisture and putrescibility: dry, low-moisture bulky waste (Trash) at one end, and wet, easily decomposable organic waste (Garbage) at the other, with Rubbish and Refuse occupying intermediate positions (Refuse specifically being defined as a combination category including garbage, giving it a higher moisture content than plain rubbish but less than pure garbage).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_b',no:38,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II [LIST-I (Convention/Protocol): A. Montreal Protocol ; B. Stockholm Convention ; C. Kyoto Protocol ; D. Basel Convention]<br>[LIST-II (Related With): I. Global Warming ; II. Transboundary movements of hazardous waste ; III. Ozone layer depletion ; IV. Persistent organic pollutants] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','B) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','C) A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II','D) A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I'],ans:1,exp:'Each of these major international environmental treaties addresses a specific, distinct environmental problem: the Montreal Protocol targets ozone-depleting substances; the Stockholm Convention targets persistent organic pollutants (long-lived toxic chemicals); the Kyoto Protocol targets greenhouse gas emissions driving global warming/climate change; and the Basel Convention targets the safe, controlled movement and disposal of hazardous waste across international borders.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_b',no:39,q:'Which of the following renewable energy resources contributes the highest proportion to the total installed power capacity in India?',opts:['A) Solar','B) Nuclear','C) Wind','D) Hydro'],ans:0,exp:'India\'s renewable energy capacity mix has transformed rapidly: historically, large Hydro power was the dominant renewable source for decades, but sustained, large-scale investment under India\'s National Solar Mission and related schemes has driven explosive growth in solar installed capacity, which has now overtaken both Wind and Hydro to become the leading renewable contributor to India\'s total installed power capacity.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jan26_b',no:40,q:'The Black-foot disease is caused by the contamination of ________ in drinking water.',opts:['A) Arsenic','B) Cadmium','C) Mercury','D) Lead'],ans:0,exp:'Black-foot disease is a well-documented case study in environmental toxicology, historically observed in specific regions of Taiwan where groundwater contained abnormally high natural arsenic concentrations. Chronic arsenic ingestion damages peripheral blood vessels, progressively restricting blood flow to the extremities (particularly the feet), leading to gangrene and the disease\'s characteristic blackening – making arsenic contamination the well-established causative agent.'},
  // ── 31 Dec 2025 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec25_a',no:36,q:'Which of the following are characteristics of sustainable development? A. Intergenerational equity regarding natural resources. ; B. Equitable distribution of natural resources in the society. ; C. Economic development at the expense of environment. ; D. Reduction in society\'s use of non-renewable resources.',opts:['A) A and D only','B) A and B only','C) A, B and C only','D) A, B and D only'],ans:3,exp:'Sustainable development (per the Brundtland Commission definition: "development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs") rests on intergenerational equity (A true) and intragenerational/equitable distribution of resources (B true), and actively promotes reduced dependence on non-renewable resources (D true). C directly contradicts sustainable development, which explicitly seeks to balance – not sacrifice – the environment for economic growth. Correct Answer: Option D – A, B and D only'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec25_a',no:37,q:'Arrange the following in descending order of installed capacity of total solar power as of August, 2025: A. Gujarat B. Rajasthan C. Maharashtra D. Tamil Nadu',opts:['A) A, B, C, D','B) A, B, D, C','C) B, A, D, C','D) B, A, C, D'],ans:3,exp:'As of mid-to-late 2025, official/industry solar-capacity data consistently placed Rajasthan at the top (over 30 GW installed, driven by the Bhadla Solar Park – the world\'s largest fully operational solar park), followed by Gujarat (roughly 21 GW), then Maharashtra (approx. 12–13 GW), and Tamil Nadu (approx. 10–11 GW). $\\text{Rajasthan (B)} > \\text{Gujarat (A)} > \\text{Maharashtra (C)} > \\text{Tamil Nadu (D)}$ Correct Answer: Option D – B, A, C, D'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec25_a',no:38,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II: [LIST-I (Mission LiFE objectives): A. Energy saved ; B. E-waste reduced ; C. Healthy lifestyles adopted ; D. Single use plastic reduced]<br>[LIST-II (Action): I. Use menstrual cups instead of sanitary napkins ; II. Start biodiversity conservation at community level ; III. Use carpooling with friends and colleagues ; IV. Prefer cloud storage over a pen drive/hard drive]',opts:['A) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','B) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','C) A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','D) A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II'],ans:2,exp:'Mission LiFE (launched at COP26, formally rolled out in 2022) organises pro-planet individual actions under themes such as saving energy, saving water, reducing single-use plastic, adopting sustainable food systems, reducing waste, adopting healthy lifestyles, and adopting sustainable housing.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec25_a',no:39,q:'Which of the following is a criteria air pollutant as well as a greenhouse gas?',opts:['A) N$_2$O','B) CO$_2$','C) O$_3$','D) NO$_2$'],ans:2,exp:'Criteria air pollutants (as classified by regulatory bodies such as the US EPA under NAAQS) are: particulate matter, ground-level ozone (O$_3$), carbon monoxide, sulfur dioxide, nitrogen oxides, and lead. Ground-level (tropospheric) ozone is unique among these in that it is also a recognised greenhouse gas due to its radiative forcing effect on climate. CO$_2$ and N$_2$O are major greenhouse gases but are not classified as criteria pollutants under the standard NAAQS list. NO$_2$ is a criteria pollutant (as part of the NOx family) but is not itself classified among the primary greenhouse gases.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec25_a',no:40,q:'Liquid that passes through the landfill, and that has extracted dissolved and suspended matter from it is called________.',opts:['A) activated sludge','B) leachate','C) ultrafiltration','D) refuse'],ans:1,exp:'Activated sludge is a biological wastewater-treatment process; ultrafiltration is a membrane filtration technique; refuse is a general term for solid waste. The liquid that percolates through a landfill, picking up dissolved and suspended contaminants along the way, is specifically termed leachate. Correct Answer: Option B – leachate'},
  // ── 27 Jun 2025 · Morning Shift 1 ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_b',no:36,q:'As temperature increases, due to Global Warming, the release of methane from wetlands will increase, is an example of',opts:['A) Positive feedback','B) Negative feedback','C) Both positive and negative feedback','D) Neither positive nor negative feedback'],ans:0,exp:'Because methane is itself a greenhouse gas, more methane release (triggered by warming) causes further warming, which in turn releases even more methane. This self-amplifying cycle – where the effect reinforces its own cause – is the defining characteristic of a positive feedback loop, confirming Option (A). Why the others fail: (B) Would require the response to counteract warming (e.g. increased cloud cover reflecting sunlight); methane release does the opposite. (C) The described mechanism is purely reinforcing (amplifying); it has no dampening/counteracting component described in the question. (D) The scenario clearly describes a causal feedback loop (temperature $\\to$ methane $\\to$ temperature), so it cannot be classified as "neither".'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_b',no:37,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>LIST-I (Health related terms)</th><th>LIST-II (Explanation)</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>Toxicology</td><td>I.</td><td>Action taken to prevent environmental health hazard</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>Exposure Assessment</td><td>II.</td><td>Evaluation of public risk of a hazard</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>Risk Assessment</td><td>III.</td><td>Science of effect of toxic substances</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>Risk Management</td><td>IV.</td><td>Estimation of impacts of environmental contaminants on humans</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I','B) A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II','C) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','D) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I'],ans:0,exp:'Following the logical sequence of environmental health risk study – Toxicology (the science), Exposure Assessment (measuring contact), Risk Assessment (evaluating danger), Risk Management (taking preventive action) – and matching each to its precise definition gives A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I, which is Option (A). Why the others fail: (B) Correctly places A and B but swaps C and D, wrongly defining Risk Assessment as "taking preventive action" (which is actually Risk Management). (C) Follows the "natural" I-II-III-IV order rather than the true definitions; none of the four pairings is correct. (D) Wrongly defines Toxicology as "evaluation of public risk" (actually Risk Assessment) and Exposure Assessment as "science of toxic effects" (actually Toxicology).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_b',no:38,q:'Which of the following are body waves generated during an earthquake? A. P-wave B. S-wave C. Love wave D. Raleigh wave Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A and B Only','B) A, B and C Only','C) B, C and D Only','D) C and D Only'],ans:0,exp:'P-waves and S-waves both travel through the Earth\'s interior and are therefore classified as body waves, while Love waves and Rayleigh waves both travel along the Earth\'s surface and are classified as surface waves. Hence only A and B are body waves, confirming Option (A). Why the others fail: (B) Wrongly includes the Love wave, which is a surface wave, not a body wave. (C) Wrongly includes both surface waves (Love, Rayleigh) and excludes the genuine body wave P-wave. (D) Selects exactly the two surface waves and excludes both genuine body waves.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_b',no:39,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>LIST-I (Environmental Protocols/Conventions)</th><th>LIST-II (Year of action/adoption)</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>International Solar Alliance</td><td>I.</td><td>1997</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>Convention on Biodiversity</td><td>II.</td><td>2015</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>National Action Plan on Climate Change</td><td>III.</td><td>1992</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>Kyoto Protocol</td><td>IV.</td><td>2008</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I','B) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','C) A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','D) A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV'],ans:1,exp:'Matching each protocol/convention to its historically correct year – International Solar Alliance (2015), Convention on Biodiversity (1992), National Action Plan on Climate Change (2008), Kyoto Protocol (1997) – gives A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I, which is Option (B). Why the others fail: (A) Swaps A and B, wrongly dating the International Solar Alliance to 1992 and the Convention on Biodiversity to 2015. (C) Correctly places A and B but swaps C and D, wrongly dating the National Action Plan on Climate Change to 1997 and the Kyoto Protocol to 2008. (D) Mismatches all four pairs; none of the years is correctly assigned.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_b',no:40,q:'Which of the following components of Electromagnetic Radiation reach earth as a part of solar radiation? A. Gama-Rays B. X-Rays C. Ultraviolet Radiation D. Visible Rays E. Infrared Radiation Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B and C Only','B) C, D and E Only','C) A, B, D and E Only','D) A, B, C, D and E'],ans:1,exp:'Gamma rays and X-rays are almost entirely absorbed by the upper atmosphere and do not reach the Earth\'s surface in meaningful quantities, whereas Ultraviolet, Visible, and Infrared radiation do reach the surface (with UV being only partially transmitted through the ozone layer). Hence C, D and E Only is correct – Option (B). Why the others fail: (A) Wrongly includes Gamma-rays and X-rays, both of which are absorbed by the atmosphere before reaching the ground. (C) Wrongly includes Gamma-rays and X-rays and excludes Ultraviolet radiation, which does partially reach the surface. (D) Incorrectly claims that all five types of radiation reach Earth, ignoring the atmosphere\'s near-total absorption of Gamma-rays and X-rays.'},
  // ── 26 Jun 2025 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_d',no:36,q:'International day for biological diversity is observed on',opts:['A) 2 February','B) 22 April','C) 22 May','D) 16 September'],ans:2,exp:'22 May is the UN-designated International Day for Biological Diversity, chosen to raise global awareness of biodiversity issues; the other three dates in the options are genuine UN/international environmental observances but correspond to different themes (wetlands, Earth Day, ozone layer respectively), making them plausible but incorrect distractors. Why the others fail: (A) This is World Wetlands Day, not Biodiversity Day. (B) This is International Mother Earth Day (Earth Day), a related but distinct observance. (D) This is World Ozone Day, unrelated to biodiversity specifically.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_d',no:37,q:'Which of the following is a chemical treatment process in context to water treatment?',opts:['A) Sedimentation','B) Coagulation','C) Filtration','D) Grit Chamber'],ans:1,exp:'Coagulation is the only listed process that depends on a deliberate chemical reaction – adding a coagulant chemical destabilises the electrical charges on fine suspended particles so they aggregate into larger flocs that can then be removed. Sedimentation, filtration and the grit chamber, by contrast, remove impurities purely through physical mechanisms (settling under gravity, mechanical straining, and gravity-based grit removal respectively), so option (B) is the correct chemical process. Why the others fail: (A) A physical process relying on gravity to settle suspended solids; no chemical reaction is involved. (C) A physical process using a porous medium to mechanically strain out particles. (D) A physical unit that removes heavy inorganic grit through gravity settling, with no chemical reagent involved.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_d',no:38,q:'Which of the following are sinks of atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO₂)? A. Wetlands B. Oceans C. Soil D. Elephants Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A and B Only','B) C and D Only','C) A, B and C Only','D) A, B, C and D'],ans:3,exp:'Wetlands, oceans and soil are the three textbook carbon sinks, each storing carbon through a different physical or biological mechanism. The inclusion of elephants reflects newer ecological research showing that large herbivores can indirectly but measurably enhance an ecosystem\'s net carbon storage through their feeding behaviour – and the NTA\'s official key treats this indirect ecosystem-engineering role as qualifying elephants as a sink as well, making all four items (A, B, C, D) correct, i.e. option (D). Why the others fail: (A) Omits Soil (C) and Elephants (D), both of which are also credited as carbon sinks. (B) Omits Wetlands (A) and Oceans (B), the two most classical carbon sinks. (C) Omits Elephants (D), which the official key includes based on their ecosystem-engineering contribution to forest carbon storage.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_d',no:39,q:'What is the correct sequence, from smaller to larger, of following photovoltaic related systems? A. Module B. Array C. Solar cell D. Array Field Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) C, A, D, B','B) C, A, B, D','C) B, D, A, C','D) D, B, A, C'],ans:1,exp:'Applying the standard build-up hierarchy of PV systems – cells combine into modules, modules combine into arrays, and arrays combine into array fields – directly gives the smaller-to-larger sequence C, A, B, D, matching option (B). Why the others fail: (A) Places Array Field (D) before Array (B), reversing their correct relative order. (C) Starts with Array (B), skipping past the smaller Module and Solar cell units entirely. (D) This is essentially the reverse (largest-to-smallest) order, not smaller-to-larger.'},
  // ── 26 Jun 2025 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_c',no:36,q:'Which of the following are true about coliform bacteria ? A. They are used to test water contamination. B. Coliform bacteria are harmless and cause no disease. C. They are found naturally in human intestinal tract. D. Their presence in water is an indication of fecal contamination. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) A and D Only','B) B and C Only','C) A, C and D Only','D) A, B, C and D'],ans:3,exp:'Coliform bacteria are naturally occurring, generally harmless residents of the human and animal intestinal tract; because they are reliably shed in faeces, their detection in a water sample is used worldwide as a standard, practical indicator of faecal/sewage contamination. All four statements – their use in water testing (A), their harmlessness (B), their natural intestinal habitat (C), and their significance as a faecal-contamination indicator (D) – are individually and jointly correct, giving "A, B, C and D", Option (D). Why the others fail: (A) Incorrectly omits B and C, both of which are also factually correct statements about coliform bacteria\'s harmlessness and natural habitat. (B) Incorrectly omits A and D, both of which correctly describe the practical significance of coliforms as water-contamination indicators. (C) Incorrectly omits Statement B, even though it is a factually accurate statement – most coliform indicator strains genuinely are harmless and non-pathogenic.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_c',no:37,q:'Why is the concentration of CO₂ is more in the Northern Hemisphere than the Southern Hemisphere ? A. Land mass is more in the Northern Hemisphere. B. Ocean body is more in the Southern Hemisphere. C. Fossil fuel is burnt more in the Northern Hemisphere. D. Northern Hemisphere is colder than the Southern Hemisphere. Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) A and C Only','B) B, C and D Only','C) A, B and C Only','D) B and D Only'],ans:2,exp:'Three independent, complementary factors explain the Northern Hemisphere\'s higher CO₂ concentration: its larger landmass supports far greater human industrial and agricultural activity (A), which directly drives greater fossil-fuel combustion there (C), while the Southern Hemisphere\'s much larger ocean coverage provides a stronger natural carbon sink that helps offset and moderate its CO₂ levels (B). Temperature difference (D) is not a recognised causal factor in this specific phenomenon. This gives "A, B and C Only" as the correct, complete answer, Option (C). Why the others fail: (A) Correctly includes A and C but incorrectly omits B, which is also a valid, documented factor – the Southern Hemisphere\'s greater ocean coverage genuinely contributes to its lower relative CO₂ concentration through enhanced carbon absorption. (B) Incorrectly includes D (temperature difference, not a recognised causal factor) while omitting A, a genuinely valid reason related to landmass and associated human activity. (D) Incorrectly includes D while omitting both A and C, two of the three genuinely valid reasons, leaving the selection both incomplete and partly incorrect.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_c',no:38,q:'Increasing the ratio of fissile Uranium (U-235) in natural uranium is known as :',opts:['A) Enhancement','B) Concentration','C) Accumulation','D) Enrichment'],ans:3,exp:'The specific, universally recognised technical term for increasing the proportion of fissile U-235 relative to U-238 in a sample of uranium is Enrichment – this terminology is used consistently in nuclear science, energy policy, and international non-proliferation discourse (e.g. "Iran\'s uranium enrichment programme"). This directly matches Option (D). The other three options are plausible-sounding generic English words but are not the recognised technical term for this specific process. Why the others fail: (A) A generic term suggesting general improvement, not the specific, internationally standardised technical term used in nuclear science. (B) While colloquially suggestive of "increasing the proportion", this is not the formally recognised technical term used for this specific nuclear process. (C) Suggests simply gathering or building up a quantity, which does not correctly describe the isotope-ratio-changing separation process involved.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_c',no:39,q:'What is the correct sequence, from initial to final, of surface waste water treatment ? A. Disinfection B. Sedimentation C. Filtration D. Screening Choose the correct answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) A, B, C, D','B) A, B, D, C','C) D, B, A, C','D) D, B, C, A'],ans:3,exp:'Waste water treatment begins with Screening to remove large debris, followed by Sedimentation to allow suspended solids to settle, then Filtration to remove finer particulate matter, and finally Disinfection to eliminate pathogenic microorganisms before the water is released or reused. This gives the sequence D, B, C, A, exactly matching Option (D). Any alternative ordering either begins with a stage other than screening or performs disinfection before the water has been adequately clarified of solid matter. Why the others fail: (A) Incorrectly places Disinfection (A) first and Screening (D) last – the complete reverse of the logical treatment order, since disinfecting water still full of large debris and suspended solids would be ineffective and wasteful of disinfecting agents. (B) Again incorrectly places Disinfection (A) first, and additionally places Screening (D) in the third position rather than first, violating the logical requirement that large debris be removed before any other treatment step. (C) Correctly starts with Screening (D) and follows with Sedimentation (B), but incorrectly places Disinfection (A) third (before Filtration), when disinfection should logically be the final step, after all filtration is complete.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_c',no:40,q:'Which of the following are differences between Modified Mercalli Scale (MMS) and Richter Scale (RS) ? A. RS measures the energy released while MMS measures both energy and devastation B. RS uses linear scale while MMS uses logarithmic scale C. Value of RS is constant in an earthquake while MMS varies depending upon distance D. Levels in RS is more than MMS Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) A and B Only','B) B and C Only','C) C and D Only','D) A and C Only'],ans:3,exp:'The Richter Scale and Modified Mercalli Scale differ fundamentally in what they measure: RS gives a single, objective measure of energy released (magnitude), constant for a given earthquake regardless of observation location, while MMS assesses the variable, location-dependent effects and devastation experienced (intensity), which naturally decreases with distance from the epicentre. These two genuine, well-documented distinctions are captured by Statements A and C respectively, giving "A and C Only", Option (D). Statement B incorrectly reverses the scales\' mathematical nature (RS is logarithmic, not linear), and Statement D does not represent an established, accurate comparison. Why the others fail: (A) Correctly includes A but incorrectly includes B, which reverses the true mathematical nature of the Richter Scale (it is logarithmic, not linear as B implies). (B) Correctly includes C but incorrectly includes the inaccurate Statement B, while omitting the genuinely valid Statement A. (C) Correctly includes C but incorrectly includes D (not an accurate, well-established difference), while omitting the genuinely valid Statement A.'},
  // ── 25 Jun 2025 · Shift 2 ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_e',no:35,q:'Which of the following are true about pure rain water? [A. It is naturally acidic ; B. It is naturally basic ; C. It is neutral (neither acidic nor basic) ; D. Its pH value is 7 ; E. It is called as acid rain when pH becomes less than 5.6] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A and E Only','B) C and D Only','C) B Only','D) C and E Only'],ans:0,exp:'Pure rainwater is naturally mildly acidic (pH $\\approx5.6$) due to dissolved atmospheric CO$_2$ forming carbonic acid – so it is neither neutral nor pH 7, ruling out C and D, and it is certainly not basic, ruling out B. The internationally recognised threshold for classifying precipitation as “acid rain” is a pH below this natural baseline of 5.6, confirming E. Hence only A and E are correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_e',no:36,q:'In which of the following steps of hydrological cycle is water purified?',opts:['A) Condensation','B) Precipitation','C) Evaporation','D) Transpiration'],ans:2,exp:'Water purification in the hydrological cycle happens during evaporation: as liquid water turns to vapour, salts and most other impurities are left behind, so the rising vapour is naturally distilled/purified water – unlike condensation or precipitation, which can actually pick up atmospheric pollutants.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_e',no:37,q:'The scale, which not only measures the intensity of earthquake but also its impact on people, is known as',opts:['A) Richter Scale','B) Modified Mercalli Scale','C) Seismogram','D) Intensity Scale'],ans:1,exp:'The Modified Mercalli Scale evaluates earthquakes based on observed effects on people, buildings, and the natural environment at various locations, unlike the Richter Scale, which is a purely instrument-derived measure of the energy released at the earthquake\'s source.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_e',no:38,q:'Which of the following are true about moderator used in nuclear reactors? [A. Moderators slow down neutrons to sustain the chain reaction ; B. Moderators stop the neutrons to control chain reaction ; C. Without a moderator chain reaction would stop ; D. Example of moderator is Graphite Rod] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A and C Only','B) B and D Only','C) A, C and D Only','D) B, C and D Only'],ans:2,exp:'Moderators slow (not stop) fast neutrons to sustain the fission chain reaction (A, true), and without this thermalisation the chain reaction in a thermal reactor would not be sustained (C, true). Graphite is a standard, well-known moderator material (D, true). Statement B incorrectly attributes a ‘stopping\' function to moderators – that role belongs to control rods – so only A, C and D are correct.'},
  // ── 25 Jun 2025 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_a',no:36,q:'What is the correct sequence, from initial to final, of blue baby disease, methemoglobinemia, in human infants? A. Conversion of nitrate ions to nitrite ions by microorganisms in human body B. Oxidation of ions from Fe(II) to Fe(III) in hemoglobin by nitrite ions. C. Consumption of nitrate polluted water D. Forming of abnormal hemoglobin called methemoglobin Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) C, B, A, D','B) C, A, B, D','C) A, D, B, C','D) A, C, B, D'],ans:1,exp:'The disease pathway begins with ingestion of nitrate-contaminated water (C), proceeds through microbial reduction of nitrate to nitrite in the gut (A), then nitrite-driven oxidation of Fe(II) to Fe(III) in haemoglobin (B), finally resulting in the formation of methemoglobin (D), which cannot carry oxygen efficiently – the underlying cause of "blue baby" cyanosis in infants. Why the others fail: (A) incorrectly places the Fe(II)$\\to$Fe(III) oxidation (B) before the nitrate-to-nitrite conversion (A), even though oxidation by nitrite can only occur after nitrite has been produced. (C) starts with the biotransformation step before exposure has even occurred, and places the final outcome (D) before the oxidation step (B) that causes it. (D) places nitrate-to-nitrite conversion (A) before water consumption (C), reversing cause and effect.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_a',no:37,q:'Electrostatic Precipitator is used in Thermal Power Plants to remove which of the following air pollutants?',opts:['A) Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons','B) Gaseous Pollutants','C) Fly ash','D) Volatile Organic Compounds'],ans:2,exp:'Since an ESP works by electrically charging and capturing solid particles, it is effective specifically against particulate pollutants like fly ash, and not against gaseous or volatile pollutants, which pass through as gas molecules rather than charged particles. Why the others fail: (A) these are organic compounds typically controlled by combustion optimisation or specific scrubbing methods, not the primary target of an ESP. (B) ESPs act on charged particulates, not on gas-phase pollutants (e.g. $\\text{SO}_2$, $\\text{NO}_x$), which require separate scrubbers/converters. (D) VOCs remain in gaseous form and are not captured by the electrostatic-charging mechanism of an ESP.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_a',no:38,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II. <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>LIST-I (Type of Water Pollutant)</th><th>LIST-II (Example)</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>Disease Causing Agent</td><td>I.</td><td>Fine particles of clay</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>Oxygen Consuming Waste</td><td>II.</td><td>Coliform Bacteria</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>Plant Nutrient</td><td>III.</td><td>Animals and Plants in aquatic habitat</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>Suspended Solids</td><td>IV.</td><td>Phosphorous</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','B) A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I','C) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV','D) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III'],ans:1,exp:'Each pollutant category is matched to its textbook example: disease-causing agents are exemplified by coliform bacteria (II), oxygen-consuming waste is exemplified by decomposing organic matter from aquatic animals/plants (III), plant nutrients are exemplified by phosphorous (IV), and suspended solids are exemplified by fine clay particles (I). This exact combination is Option (B). Why the others fail: (A) correctly pairs A-II and B-III but swaps the examples for Plant Nutrient and Suspended Solids (C and D). (C) the "obvious" sequential pairing; none of the four pairs is actually correct. (D) mismatches A and B while correctly pairing C-IV; overall still incorrect.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_a',no:39,q:'Match the LIST-I with LIST-II. <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>LIST-I (Process)</th><th>LIST-II (Explanation)</th></tr><tr><td>A.</td><td>Ozonation</td><td>I.</td><td>Removal of excess iron and manganese</td></tr><tr><td>B.</td><td>Aeration</td><td>II.</td><td>Removal of large solids</td></tr><tr><td>C.</td><td>Flocculation</td><td>III.</td><td>Disinfection</td></tr><tr><td>D.</td><td>Screening</td><td>IV.</td><td>Very small particles are clustered together</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II','B) A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV','C) A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV','D) A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'],ans:0,exp:'Each treatment process is matched to its correct function: Ozonation for disinfection (III), Aeration for removing dissolved iron/manganese (I), Flocculation for clustering fine particles (IV), and Screening for removing large solids at the very start of the process (II). This exact combination is Option (A). Why the others fail: (B) correctly pairs A and B but swaps the functions of Flocculation and Screening (C and D). (C) swaps the functions of Ozonation and Aeration (A and B), and also swaps C and D. (D) a fully scrambled mapping; none of the four pairs is correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun25_a',no:40,q:'What is the correct increasing order of U-235 (fissile uranium) ratio in following? A. Natural Uranium B. Uranium Ore C. Weapon grade uranium D. Reactor grade uranium Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) B, A, C, D','B) B, A, D, C','C) D, C, A, B','D) D, C, B, A'],ans:1,exp:'Moving from the least processed to the most highly enriched form: Uranium Ore and Natural Uranium sit at the bottom with the natural $\\sim0.7\\%$ U-235 ratio, Reactor Grade uranium is enriched to a few percent for power generation, and Weapon Grade uranium is enriched to over $90\\%$ – giving the increasing order B, A, D, C. Why the others fail: (A) incorrectly places Weapon Grade (C) before Reactor Grade (D), reversing their relative enrichment levels. (C) lists the grades in fully decreasing order of enrichment, the reverse of what is asked. (D) also a decreasing-order sequence, and additionally swaps the relative position of Uranium Ore and Natural Uranium.'},
  // ── Dec 2024 · Set 1 ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec24_a',no:35,q:'Which one of the following is used as a reference to measure the Global Warming Potential (GWP) of a green house gas?',opts:['A) Carbon dioxide (CO$_2$)','B) Methane (CH$_4$)','C) Chlorofluorocarbon (CFC)','D) Hydrofluorocarbon (HFC)'],ans:0,exp:'Carbon dioxide is the universally adopted baseline gas (GWP $=1$) against which the warming potential of every other greenhouse gas, including methane, CFCs and HFCs, is expressed, confirming Option (A). Global Warming Potential (GWP) measures how much heat a greenhouse gas traps in the atmosphere relative to a reference gas, over a specified time horizon (commonly 100 years).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec24_a',no:36,q:'Sustainable Development Goal 7 is related to.',opts:['A) Gender Equality','B) Climate Action','C) Affordable and Clean energy','D) Quality Education'],ans:2,exp:'By direct recall of the official UN numbering, SDG 7 is specifically “Affordable and Clean Energy,” distinguishing it from the other listed goals which carry different official numbers. The UN\'s 17 Sustainable Development Goals (2015–2030) each address a distinct global priority. Relevant to this item: SDG 4 = Quality Education; SDG 5 = Gender Equality; SDG 7 = Affordable and Clean Energy; SDG 13 = Climate Action.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec24_a',no:37,q:'Toxic substances that damage kidneys are called as.',opts:['A) Hematotoxic','B) Neurotoxic','C) Cytotoxic','D) Nephrotoxic'],ans:3,exp:'The medical/toxicological prefix “nephro-” denotes the kidney, making “nephrotoxic” the precise and correct term for substances that specifically damage kidneys, matching Option (D). Toxicology classifies toxins by the organ/system they primarily damage: Nephrotoxic substances damage the kidneys (nephron structures); Neurotoxic substances damage the nervous system; Hematotoxic substances damage blood/blood-forming tissue;'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec24_a',no:38,q:'Montreal Protocol is related to protection of.',opts:['A) Biodiversity','B) Glaciers','C) Oceans','D) Stratospheric ozone layer'],ans:3,exp:'The Montreal Protocol\'s entire purpose, since its adoption in 1987, is to eliminate substances that deplete the ozone layer, directly matching Option (D) and distinguishing it from other environmental treaties addressing different concerns. The syllabus explicitly lists “Montreal Protocol” among the international agreements under Unit IX. The Montreal Protocol (1987) is a landmark international treaty designed to phase out the production and consumption of substances (like CFCs) responsible for depleting the…'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec24_a',no:39,q:'Which of the following are examples of organic pollutants in water. (i) Salts ; (ii) Pesticides ; (iii) Dioxins ; (iv) Bases ; (v) Pigments Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (i), (ii) and (iii) only','B) (ii), (iii) and (iv) only','C) (iii), (iv) and (v) only','D) (ii), (iii) and (v) only'],ans:3,exp:'Pesticides, dioxins and pigments are all carbon-based (organic) compounds, whereas salts and bases are inorganic chemical species – so exactly (ii), (iii) and (v) qualify as organic water pollutants, matching Option (D). Water pollutants are broadly classified as inorganic (e.g., salts, acids, bases, heavy-metal ions – lacking carbon-based/organic structure) or organic (carbon-based compounds, often from synthetic chemical or biological sources, e.g., pesticides, dioxins, dyes/pigments,…'},
  // ── 29 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_g',no:36,q:'Which of the following are routes for exposure to pollutants in humans ? (A) Inhalation ; (B) Consumption ; (C) Ingestion ; (D) Dermal ; (E) Digestion Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (A), (B) and (C) Only','B) (A), (C) and (D) Only','C) (B), (D) and (E) Only','D) (A), (C) and (E) Only'],ans:1,exp:'Environmental toxicology recognises exactly three standard routes of human exposure to pollutants: Inhalation, Ingestion, and Dermal contact. “Consumption” and “Digestion” are not the precise technical terms for a distinct exposure pathway. Hence “(A), (C) and (D) Only” – Option (B) – is correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_g',no:37,q:'Which one of the following is considered as a prominent indoor air pollutant ?',opts:['A) Mercury','B) Radon','C) Helium','D) Argon'],ans:1,exp:'Radon is a naturally occurring radioactive gas that seeps into buildings from underlying soil/rock and is internationally recognised as a leading indoor air pollutant and cause of lung cancer. Hence Option (B) is correct. Radon is a naturally occurring, colourless, odourless radioactive gas produced by the decay of uranium present in soil and rock. It can seep into buildings through foundations/cracks and accumulate indoors, where poor ventilation allows it to reach harmful concentrations;'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_g',no:38,q:'Leachate is a :',opts:['A) Green house gas','B) Air pollutant','C) Liquid extract from landfill site','D) Ozone depleting substance'],ans:2,exp:'Leachate is, by definition, the contaminated liquid produced when water percolates through and extracts pollutants from landfill waste – exactly matching Option (C). Leachate is the liquid that forms when water (rainfall or moisture within the waste) percolates through solid waste in a landfill, dissolving and carrying with it a range of contaminants – organic matter, heavy metals, and other pollutants – from the decomposing waste.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_g',no:39,q:'Which of the following are true about tropospheric or ground level ozone ? (A) It is called as good ozone ; (B) It is formed due to downward transfer of ozone from ozone layer ; (C) It is formed due to the action of sunlight on oxides of nitrogen ; (D) It is a green house gas ; (E) It is a key component of photochemical smog Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (B), (C), (D) and (E) Only','B) (A), (B) and (C) Only','C) (C), (D) and (E) Only','D) (A), (D) and (E) Only'],ans:0,exp:'Tropospheric ozone is “bad ozone” (not good), so Statement A is false. It genuinely (i) receives a minor natural contribution via downward transfer from the stratosphere, (ii) forms predominantly through the photochemical action of sunlight on nitrogen oxides, (iii) acts as a greenhouse gas, and (iv) is a key component of photochemical smog – making B, C, D, and E all true. Hence Option (A) is correct.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_g',no:40,q:'Which of the following are true about International Solar Alliance (ISA) ? (A) There are over 100 member countries in this alliance ; (B) Its head quarter is in Geneva, Switzerland ; (C) Most of its member countries lie between equator and tropic of cancer ; (D) Primary objective of alliance is to efficiently use solar energy ; (E) It is a collaborative initiative of India and France Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (A), (B) and (D) Only','B) (A), (D) and (E) Only','C) (B), (C) and (E) Only','D) (C), (D) and (E) Only'],ans:1,exp:'ISA has over 100 member countries (A – true), its core objective is efficient solar energy deployment (D – true), and it is a joint India–France initiative (E – true). Its headquarters is in Gurugram, India, not Geneva (B – false), and its target countries span between the Tropic of Cancer and the Tropic of Capricorn, not merely equator-to-Cancer (C – false/imprecise). Hence “(A), (D) and (E) Only” – Option (B) – is correct.'},
  // ── 28 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_f',no:36,q:'Excess presence of which one of the following in drinking water can elevate the blood pressure of humans?',opts:['A) Sulphate','B) Zinc','C) Lead','D) Copper'],ans:2,exp:'Among the four options, lead is the contaminant with a well-established, specific link to elevated blood pressure in humans through chronic exposure, making option (C) correct; the other three metals/ions are associated with different primary health effects. Heavy-metal contamination of drinking water is a well-documented environmental health hazard. Lead (Pb) is specifically and extensively linked in toxicological and epidemiological literature to elevated blood pressure (hypertension) in humans, alongside its other neurotoxic…'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_f',no:37,q:'Match List - I with List - II. List - I (Water treatment) List - II (Process) <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>(A) Primary</th><th>(I) Removal of wastes with the help of activated charcoal etc.</th></tr><tr><td>(B) Secondary</td><td>(II) Ozonization and disinfection by ultraviolet rays.</td></tr><tr><td>(C) Tertiary</td><td>(III) Removal of large pieces such as stick, stone, rags, plastic etc.</td></tr><tr><td>(D) Advanced</td><td>(IV) Removal after flocculation and coagulation of wastes.</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)','B) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','C) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','D) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)'],ans:1,exp:'Each treatment stage is matched to its textbook-standard process: Primary removes large debris, Secondary uses flocculation/coagulation, Tertiary uses activated-charcoal filtration, and Advanced treatment finishes with ozonization/UV disinfection – exactly option (B).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_f',no:38,q:'Which of the following is/are Environmental Hazard(s) due to Geothermal Power Plants? (A) Noise pollution (B) Production of SO$_2$ and NO$_X$ (C) Production of H$_2$S (toxic gas) (D) Production of CO$_2$ Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A) Only','B) (B) and (D) Only','C) (C) Only','D) (A), (B), (C) and (D)'],ans:3,exp:'Geothermal power generation, despite being comparatively clean, is documented to cause noise pollution and to release a range of gases – SO$_2$, NO$_X$, CO$_2$, and notably H$_2$S – that are entrained in the geothermal steam/fluid extracted from underground. Since all four listed hazards are genuine and documented, the correct answer includes all of them – option (D).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_f',no:39,q:'Which of the following indices represents the background level of noise pollution?',opts:['A) $L_{10}$','B) $L_{25}$','C) $L_{50}$','D) $L_{90}$'],ans:3,exp:'Because $L_{90}$ is the sound level exceeded for 90% of the measurement period, it reflects the noise that is almost always present – the ambient/background level – rather than occasional loud (intrusive) events, which is why environmental-acoustics practice designates $L_{90}$ as the background noise index, matching option (D).'},
  // ── 23 Aug 2024 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_d',no:34,q:'The amount of rainfall required to saturate the soil is known as.',opts:['A) Field Capacity','B) Soil Moisture content','C) Soil Moisture deficit','D) Wilting Coefficient'],ans:2,exp:'The question specifically asks for an amount of rainfall, which points to a quantity/deficit concept rather than a static soil-moisture state. Soil Moisture Deficit is precisely defined as the volume of water that must be added (via rainfall or irrigation) to bring the soil back up to its field-capacity (saturated) level, making it the term that exactly matches the question\'s phrasing.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_d',no:35,q:'Groundwater is specially prone to excessive hardness due to.',opts:['A) Calcium and Sodium ions','B) Calcium and Magnesium ions','C) Sodium and Magnesium ions','D) Potassium and Calcium ions'],ans:1,exp:'Groundwater\'s prolonged contact with subsurface rock and soil (compared to surface water) allows it to dissolve significant quantities of calcium and magnesium carbonates/bicarbonates and sulfates from limestone, dolomite, and gypsum-bearing strata, making it especially prone to hardness. These two divalent cations are universally recognised in water chemistry as the defining cause of “hard water.”'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_d',no:36,q:'Arrange the following events in the order of their occurrence starting from oldest to the recent. A. Bhopal gas tragedy ; B. Fukushima disaster ; C. Chernobil disaster ; D. California Smog ; E. London Smog Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) D, E, B, A, C','B) E, C, A, B, D','C) D, E, A, C, B','D) E, D, A, B, C'],ans:2,exp:'Each of these five events is a well-documented, dateable environmental disaster spanning nearly seven decades of industrial and nuclear history: from the earliest photochemical smog episodes in 1940s Los Angeles, through the infamous 1952 London smog, to the catastrophic Bhopal gas leak of 1984, the Chernobyl nuclear disaster of 1986, and finally the Fukushima nuclear disaster of 2011 – giving the strict chronological order D, E, A, C, B.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_d',no:37,q:'Which of the following National missions come under the National Action Plan for climate change? A. National Solar mission ; B. National Water mission ; C. National Green Hydrogen mission ; D. National Mission for Sustainable Agriculture ; E. National Mission on Sustainable Habitat Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C and D Only','B) B, C and E Only','C) C, D, E and A Only','D) D, E, A and B Only'],ans:3,exp:'Four of the five listed missions (Solar, Water, Sustainable Agriculture, Sustainable Habitat) are genuinely among the eight missions constituting the 2008 NAPCC framework. The National Green Hydrogen Mission, though an important and topical climate-related government initiative, was launched separately in 2023 – 15 years after NAPCC – and was never one of its original eight missions, making it the correct item to exclude.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_d',no:38,q:'Match the List-I with List-II [LIST I Organism: A. Lion ; B. Trees ; C. Fish ; D. Grasshopper]<br>[LIST II Trophic Level: I. First Trophic level ; II. Second Trophic Level ; III. Third Trophic level ; IV. Fourth Trophic level] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III','B) A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II','C) A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV','D) A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I'],ans:1,exp:'Assigning each organism its correct position in the standard producer-to-apex-predator trophic hierarchy gives: Trees (producer, Level I), Grasshopper (herbivore, Level II), Fish (secondary consumer, Level III), and Lion (top/apex consumer, Level IV). This full sequence, A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II, is given precisely by Option B.'},
  // ── 23 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_c',no:35,q:'The amount of biomass produced by the plants through photosynthesis minus respiration losses in a given time period is known as:',opts:['A) Gross Primary Productivity','B) Net Primary Productivity','C) Gross Ecological Productivity','D) Net Secondary Productivity'],ans:1,exp:'Gross Primary Productivity (GPP) is the total rate of biomass (organic matter) production by producers (plants) through photosynthesis. Since plants also respire and use up some of this energy, Net Primary Productivity (NPP) $=$ GPP $-$ Respiration (R). NPP represents the biomass actually available to consumers (herbivores) in the ecosystem.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_c',no:36,q:'Which among the following fuels produces least amount of soot particles during combustion?',opts:['A) Petrol','B) Kerosene','C) Diesel','D) Compressed Natural Gas (CNG)'],ans:3,exp:'Soot (particulate carbon) forms mainly from incomplete combustion of carbon-rich liquid/solid hydrocarbon fuels. CNG (Compressed Natural Gas), being predominantly methane (a simple gaseous hydrocarbon), burns more completely and cleanly than liquid fuels such as petrol, kerosene or diesel, producing significantly less particulate matter/soot – this is a key reason CNG is promoted as a cleaner transport fuel.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_c',no:37,q:'Arrange the following states in ascending order according to their area under forest cover: (A) Arunachal Pradesh ; (B) Chhattisgarh ; (C) Maharastra ; (D) Odisha ; (E) Madhya Pradesh Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (C), (D), (B), (E)','B) (C), (D), (B), (A), (E)','C) (B), (A), (C), (E), (D)','D) (D), (B), (A), (E), (C)'],ans:1,exp:'The India State of Forest Report (ISFR), published periodically by the Forest Survey of India, ranks Indian states by total forest cover area. Among Indian states, Madhya Pradesh consistently has the largest forest cover area, followed by Arunachal Pradesh, Chhattisgarh, Odisha, and Maharashtra in the top-five range (exact ordering can shift slightly between report editions, but this relative pattern is the one tested here).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_c',no:38,q:'Eye irritation is the common phenomenon during smog. It is mostly caused by: (A) Formaldehyde ; (B) Per-oxy acetyl Nitrate ; (C) Ozone ; (D) Acrolin Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (B) and (C) only','B) (A), (B) and (D) only','C) (C), (D) and (A) only','D) (B), (C) and (D) only'],ans:1,exp:'Photochemical smog forms when sunlight reacts with pollutants such as nitrogen oxides and volatile organic compounds, producing secondary pollutants including Peroxyacetyl Nitrate (PAN), Formaldehyde, Acrolein, and Ozone. Among these, PAN, Formaldehyde and Acrolein are specifically well-documented as strong eye irritants, whereas Ozone\'s principal health impact is on the respiratory system (lungs and airways) rather than being the chief cause of eye irritation.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_c',no:39,q:'Match List - I with List - II. <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th>List - I (International Treaties)</th><th>List - II (Key Objectives)</th></tr><tr><td>(A) Sendai Framework</td><td>(I) Equality between men and women</td></tr><tr><td>(B) Monterrey Consensus</td><td>(II) Our common future</td></tr><tr><td>(C) Beijing Declaration</td><td>(III) Disaster Risk Reduction</td></tr><tr><td>(D) Brundtland Report</td><td>(IV) Financing for development</td></tr></table></div> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)','B) (A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)','C) (A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)','D) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)'],ans:0,exp:'The Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction (2015–2030) is the global agreement guiding disaster-risk reduction efforts. The Monterrey Consensus (2002) is an international agreement focused on financing for development. The Beijing Declaration (Fourth World Conference on Women, 1995) is centred on gender equality and women\'s empowerment. The Brundtland Report ("Our Common Future," 1987) introduced the modern definition of sustainable development.'},
  // ── 22 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_e',no:36,q:'Based on the source of their food, organisms occupy a specific place in the food chain, known as :',opts:['A) Trophic level','B) Standing crop','C) Top level organism','D) Species level'],ans:0,exp:'Since the question specifically describes the position of an organism in the food chain based on its food source, this exactly matches the definition of a ‘trophic level\' – Option (A). In ecology, a trophic level refers to the specific position an organism occupies in a food chain, determined by the source and number of transfers of its energy/food – e.g., producers occupy the first trophic level, primary consumers (herbivores) the second, secondary consumers…'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_e',no:37,q:'Which of the following is not a primary air pollutant ?',opts:['A) CO','B) NO','C) O$_3$','D) SO$_2$'],ans:2,exp:'Since CO, NO, and SO$_2$ are all directly emitted from combustion sources (making them primary pollutants), while ground-level ozone (O$_3$) is instead formed through photochemical reactions in the atmosphere (making it a secondary pollutant), Option (C) is the correct answer.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_e',no:38,q:'Arrange the following gases in ascending order of their global warming potential (GWD). (A) CO$_2$ ; (B) CH$_4$ ; (C) SF$_6$ ; (D) CFC-11 ; (E) N$_2$O Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (A), (B) (C), (E), (D)','B) (A), (B) (E), (D), (C)','C) (B), (C) (A), (E), (D)','D) (A), (E) (B), (D), (C)'],ans:1,exp:'Using standard reference GWP values, the ascending order is CO$_2$ (lowest, baseline) $<$ CH$_4$ $<$ N$_2$O $<$ CFC-11 $<$ SF$_6$ (highest) – i.e., A, B, E, D, C, exactly Option (B). Global Warming Potential (GWP) measures how much heat a greenhouse gas traps in the atmosphere relative to the same mass of CO$_2$ (which is defined as the baseline, GWP $=1$) over a given time horizon (typically 100 years).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_e',no:39,q:'Which among the followings are global scale Phenomenon ? (A) Ozone layer depletion ; (B) Global warming ; (C) Forest Fire ; (D) Sea level rise ; (E) Air pollution Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (A), (C) and (D) Only','B) (A), (B), (C) and (D) Only','C) (B), (C) and (E) Only','D) (A), (B) and (D) Only'],ans:3,exp:'Ozone layer depletion, global warming, and sea level rise are all genuinely planetary-scale phenomena (A, B, D), whereas forest fires and air pollution are predominantly local/regional in their direct impact (C, E) – so the correct set is A, B, D, Option (D). Environmental issues can be classified by their spatial scale of impact: global-scale phenomena affect the entire planet regardless of the location of their source (e.g., ozone layer depletion, global warming, sea level rise, since greenhouse gases and ozone-depleting substances…'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_e',no:40,q:'Match List - I with List - II. [List - I: A. Kyoto protocol ; B. Montreal protocol ; C. Rio Summit ; D. Paris Agreement]<br>[List - II: I. Global warming ; II. Biodiversity conservation ; III. Ozone depletion ; IV. Solar Alliance] Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)','B) (A)-(II), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)','C) (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)','D) (A)-(IV), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(III)'],ans:0,exp:'The Kyoto Protocol targets global warming (I); the Montreal Protocol targets ozone depletion (III); the Rio Summit is linked to biodiversity conservation (II); and the Paris Agreement is associated with the launch of the International Solar Alliance (IV). This gives A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV – Option (A).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_e',no:44,q:'Arrange the following themes of Sustainable Development Goals according to their goal number in increasing order. (A) Gender Equality ; (B) Zero Hunger ; (C) Peace and Justice ; (D) Climate Action ; (E) Quality Education Choose the correct answer from the options given below :',opts:['A) (B), (E) (A), (D), (C)','B) (A), (B) (D), (C), (E)','C) (C), (D) (A), (E), (B)','D) (B), (A) (E), (D), (C)'],ans:0,exp:'Assigning each theme its correct SDG number (Zero Hunger=2, Quality Education=4, Gender Equality=5, Climate Action=13, Peace and Justice=16) and arranging in increasing order gives B, E, A, D, C – exactly Option (A). The United Nations\' 17 Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs, adopted 2015) are each assigned a fixed goal number. Relevant numbers here: SDG 2 – Zero Hunger; SDG 4 – Quality Education; SDG 5 – Gender Equality; SDG 13 – Climate Action;'},
  // ── 21 Aug 2024 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_b',no:35,q:'Which of the following Green House Gases (GHGs) was considered during the second compliance period of Kyoto protocol at Doha?',opts:['A) Sulphur Hexafluoride (SF$_6$)','B) Hydrofluoro Carbons (HFCs)','C) Nitrogen Trifluoride (NF$_3$)','D) Perfluorocarbons (PFCs)'],ans:2,exp:'While SF$_6$, HFCs, and PFCs were already part of the Kyoto Protocol\'s original six-gas basket since 1997, Nitrogen Trifluoride (NF$_3$) was specifically added as a new controlled gas under the Doha Amendment governing the Protocol\'s second commitment period. This question is from Unit IX: People, Development and Environment – International agreements/efforts (Kyoto Protocol). The original Kyoto Protocol (1997) covered a basket of six greenhouse gases: CO$_2$, CH$_4$, N$_2$O, HFCs, PFCs, and SF$_6$.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_b',no:36,q:'Match the List-I with List-II 1.3 [LIST I (Particulate Matter Terms): A. Aerosol ; B. Nano Particles ; C. Smoke ; D. Fine particles]<br>[LIST II (Explanation): I. Particles formed by incomplete combustion with mixture of carbon volatiles ; II. Particles with size less than 2.5 micro meter ; III. Particles with size less than 0.2 micrometer ; IV. Any solid or liquid particle suspended in air] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','B) A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II','C) A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III','D) A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV'],ans:1,exp:'Aerosol is the general umbrella term for any suspended particle, Nano Particles are defined by their ultra-small ($<0.2\\,\\mu$m) size, Smoke specifically results from incomplete combustion, and Fine particles correspond to the well-known PM$_{2.5}$ air-quality standard – giving the pairing A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_b',no:37,q:'Which of the following is an example of negative feed back in the context of climate change?',opts:['A) Oceans remove carbon dioxide from the air','B) Warming increases water vapour in air','C) Snow cover loss and iceshelf melt reduce sun\'s reflection','D) Global temperature increases due to global warming'],ans:0,exp:'Because oceanic absorption of CO$_2$ actively removes a greenhouse gas and thereby reduces (dampens) the warming trend, it is the correct example of a negative feedback, unlike the other three options, which all represent processes that amplify (positive feedback) or simply describe the warming itself.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_b',no:38,q:'Burning of solid waste, such as cans, batteries, plastic etc. generate. A. Microplastics B. Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) C. Dioxins D. Polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs) E. Halons Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C and D only','B) A, C and D only','C) B, D and E only','D) B, C and E only'],ans:1,exp:'Burning plastic and mixed solid waste is a recognised source of microplastics, dioxins, and PCBs through incomplete/uncontrolled combustion, whereas CFCs and Halons are pre-manufactured chemical products released through use or leakage rather than generated by the burning process itself.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_b',no:39,q:'Match the List-I with List-II 1.3 [LIST I (Nature of substances): A. Allergens ; B. Carcinogens ; C. Mutagens ; D. Teratogens]<br>[LIST II (Effect): I. Substances that cause cancer ; II. Substances that can hamper embryonic growth ; III. Substances that activate the immune system ; IV. Substances that can damage or alter genetic material] Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III','B) A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III','C) A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV','D) A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II'],ans:3,exp:'Each term is defined by a distinct toxicological mechanism – Allergens trigger immune responses, Carcinogens cause cancer, Mutagens alter genetic material, and Teratogens disrupt embryonic development – giving the pairing A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II. This question is from Unit IX: People, Development and Environment – Impacts of pollutants on human health, testing the definitions of four categories of harmful substances classified by their biological mechanism of harm.'},
  // ── 21 Aug 2024 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_a',no:34,q:'The convention of Biological Diversity (CBD) was first opened for signature during',opts:['A) Conference on human environment','B) Earth Summit','C) Montreal Protocol','D) COP1'],ans:1,exp:'The Convention on Biological Diversity was formally opened for signature during the 1992 Rio de Janeiro Earth Summit (UNCED), distinguishing it from the earlier Stockholm conference, the separate Montreal Protocol treaty, and the CBD\'s own later first Conference of the Parties (COP1), confirming Option B.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_a',no:35,q:'To understand the Global Climate Change, GCM model are widely used. GCM stands for A. Global Circulation Model ; B. General Circulation Model ; C. Global Climate Model ; D. General Climate Model Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) Only A and B','B) Only B and C','C) Only C and D','D) Only A and D'],ans:1,exp:'Climate science literature recognises ‘General Circulation Model\' and ‘Global Climate Model\' as the two standard, valid expansions of GCM, while the cross-permutations ‘Global Circulation Model\' and ‘General Climate Model\' are not the conventionally used terms, confirming Option B (B and C).'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_a',no:36,q:'Dissolved Oxygen (DO), a water quality parameter, is essential in which of the following water bodies/sources? A. River Water ; B. Lake Water ; C. Tap Water ; D. Underground Water ; E. Pond Water Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B and E Only','B) C and D Only','C) B, D and E Only','D) A, C, D and E Only'],ans:0,exp:'Dissolved Oxygen is ecologically essential specifically for open surface water bodies that support aquatic life and are exposed to the atmosphere – rivers, lakes, and ponds (A, B, E) – while tap water and underground water (C, D) are not standard contexts in which DO functions as a critical monitored parameter, confirming Option A.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_a',no:37,q:'In a breeder reactor',opts:['A) More fissile materials are produced than it is consumed','B) Less fissile materials are produced than it is consumed','C) Neutrons are slowed down by moderator','D) U-238 a fissile material is produced from Pu-239 a fertile material.'],ans:0,exp:'By definition, a breeder reactor achieves a net positive balance of fissile material – producing more fissile fuel (via fertile-to-fissile conversion) than it consumes to sustain its own chain reaction – which is exactly what distinguishes it from a conventional reactor, confirming Option A.'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'aug24_a',no:38,q:'When a water body becomes extremely low in nutrient content, then it is called as',opts:['A) Dystrophic','B) Oligotrophic','C) Mesotrophic','D) Eutrophic'],ans:1,exp:'\'Oligotrophic\' is the precise, standard ecological term for a water body that is extremely low in nutrient content and correspondingly low in biological productivity, clearly distinguishing it from the moderate (mesotrophic), high-nutrient (eutrophic), and organic-matter-based (dystrophic) classifications, confirming Option B.'},
  // ── 11 Dec 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_e',no:36,q:'When a water body is deficient in nutrients, it is known as',opts:['A) Oligotrophic','B) Eutrophic','C) Mesotrophic','D) Dystrophic'],ans:0,alt:[3],exp:'<div class="p1-note">ℹ NTA accepted more than one option as correct for this question (Option A and Option D).</div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Water bodies are classified along a nutrient/productivity gradient: <b>Oligotrophic</b> lakes are clear, low in nutrients, and have low biological productivity – the standard, primary definition of a “nutrient-deficient” water body. <b>Eutrophic</b> lakes are nutrient-rich with high productivity (often due to pollution/runoff). <b>Mesotrophic</b> lakes fall in between. <b>Dystrophic</b> lakes are also low in <i>inorganic</i> nutrients and have low productivity, but are characteristically acidic, brownish, and rich in <b>humic/organic matter</b> (often bog-associated) – a special-case, low-productivity water body that some textbooks also describe as nutrient-poor.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Four trophic classifications of water bodies are given, and the question asks which describes nutrient deficiency.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Identify the term(s) that describe a nutrient-deficient water body.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Recall the standard trophic-state classification scale used in limnology (the study of inland waters).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Evaluate Oligotrophic:</b> Defined precisely as low in nutrients, clear water, low productivity – the textbook-standard answer for “nutrient deficient.”</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Evaluate Eutrophic and Mesotrophic:</b> Eutrophic means nutrient-<i>rich</i> (the opposite of deficient); Mesotrophic means an intermediate nutrient level – neither matches “deficient.”</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Evaluate Dystrophic:</b> Also characterised by low productivity and low inorganic nutrient availability (though rich in organic/humic matter), leading some sources to also classify it as nutrient-poor – creating genuine textbook ambiguity with Oligotrophic.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Official outcome:</b> Because standard reference material supports classifying <i>both</i> Oligotrophic and Dystrophic water bodies as nutrient-deficient/low-productivity, NTA accepted both Option 1 and Option 4 as correct.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Oligotrophic is the primary, most widely taught definition of a nutrient-deficient water body (clear, low-nutrient, low-productivity). However, Dystrophic water bodies are also low in productivity and inorganic nutrients (despite being rich in organic/humic content), and some standard environmental-science textbooks describe them as nutrient-poor as well. Because of this genuine overlap in textbook definitions, NTA accepted both Option 1 and Option 4 in the final key.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Accepted)</b></span> – Oligotrophic is the standard, primary definition of a nutrient-deficient water body.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Eutrophic describes a nutrient-<i>rich</i>, highly productive water body – the opposite of nutrient deficiency.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Mesotrophic describes an intermediate nutrient level, neither rich nor deficient.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Also Accepted)</b></span> – Dystrophic water bodies are also low-productivity/low-inorganic-nutrient systems, justifying its acceptance alongside Oligotrophic.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Trophic gradient (low to high nutrients/productivity): Oligotrophic $\\to$ Mesotrophic $\\to$ Eutrophic.</li><li>Dystrophic: acidic, humic-rich, low productivity – also sometimes classed as nutrient-poor.</li><li><b>Both Oligotrophic and Dystrophic were accepted</b> as correct by NTA for this question.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_e',no:37,q:'Discovery of Ozone hole was first made over',opts:['A) Arctic','B) Equator','C) Antarctic','D) Tropics'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The <b>ozone hole</b> was first discovered in <b>1985</b> by scientists of the <b>British Antarctic Survey</b> (Joseph Farman, Brian Gardiner, Jonathan Shanklin), who observed a dramatic seasonal depletion of stratospheric ozone over <b>Antarctica</b> (specifically near Halley Bay). This discovery was a key trigger for the international <b>Montreal Protocol (1987)</b>, which phased out ozone-depleting substances such as CFCs.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Four possible geographic locations for the first discovery of the ozone hole are given.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Identify the correct location of the original ozone hole discovery.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Recall the well-documented environmental history of ozone-hole discovery.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Recall the historical fact:</b> The dramatic ozone depletion was first documented in 1985 by British Antarctic Survey scientists over Antarctica.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Eliminate Arctic:</b> While a (much smaller and less severe) ozone thinning also occurs over the Arctic, it was <i>not</i> the location of the original, historic 1985 discovery.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Eliminate Equator and Tropics:</b> Ozone depletion of this dramatic magnitude is a polar stratospheric phenomenon (linked to polar stratospheric clouds and extreme cold), not an equatorial/tropical one.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Antarctic, Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The historic 1985 discovery of severe stratospheric ozone depletion – what came to be called the “ozone hole” – was made over Antarctica by British Antarctic Survey researchers, driven by unique polar stratospheric chemistry (extreme cold, polar stratospheric clouds, and CFC-derived chlorine). This makes Antarctic, Option 3, the correct answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Arctic does experience some ozone thinning, but it was not the site of the original 1985 discovery, and its depletion is typically less severe than Antarctica’s.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Equator is not associated with significant ozone depletion, which is primarily a polar phenomenon.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Antarctic is the historically and scientifically correct location of the original ozone hole discovery (1985).</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Tropics are not linked to ozone hole formation, which requires the extreme cold and polar stratospheric clouds found only at the poles.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Ozone hole first discovered in <b>1985</b> over <b>Antarctica</b> (British Antarctic Survey).</li><li>Triggered the international <b>Montreal Protocol (1987)</b>.</li><li>Ozone depletion is a polar (not equatorial/tropical) stratospheric phenomenon.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_e',no:38,q:'What is the correct increasing order of prescribed limits for following pollutants on 24 hours basis in residential areas by CPCB? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> Suspended Particulate Matter (SPM)</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> Respirable Suspended Particulate Matter (RSPM)</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> Lead (pb)</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> Ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>)</li></ol> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the option given below:',opts:['A) (A), (B), (C), (D).','B) (B), (A), (C), (D).','C) (C), (D), (B), (A).','D) (C), (B), (A), (D).'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The <b>Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB)</b> prescribes 24-hour National Ambient Air Quality Standards (NAAQS) for residential areas. Approximate prescribed limits (in $\\mu$g/m<sup>3</sup>): <b>Lead</b> $\\approx 1.0$ (extremely low, as lead is highly toxic even in trace amounts), <b>RSPM/PM<sub>10</sub></b> $\\approx 100$, <b>SPM</b> $\\approx 200$, and <b>Ammonia (NH<sub>3</sub>)</b> $\\approx 400$. Being aware of the relative <i>toxicity</i> of each pollutant helps predict the ordering: the most acutely toxic pollutants (like lead) are given the tightest (lowest) limits.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Four pollutants (SPM, RSPM, Lead, Ammonia) with CPCB-prescribed 24-hour residential-area limits.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Arrange the four pollutants in increasing order of their prescribed limit values.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Recall (or reason from relative toxicity) the approximate CPCB limit for each pollutant.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Recall/estimate each limit:</b> Lead $\\approx1\\,\\mu g/m^3$ (very tightly regulated due to high toxicity); RSPM $\\approx100\\,\\mu g/m^3$; SPM $\\approx200\\,\\mu g/m^3$; Ammonia $\\approx400\\,\\mu g/m^3$ (a relatively less acutely toxic gaseous pollutant with a higher permissible limit).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Arrange in increasing order:</b> Lead (C) $&lt;$ RSPM (B) $&lt;$ SPM (A) $&lt;$ Ammonia (D).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Match to answer choices:</b> This ordering corresponds exactly to (C), (B), (A), (D).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Option 4 – (C), (B), (A), (D).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Since Lead is regulated at an extremely low threshold due to its high toxicity even at trace exposure, it has the smallest permissible limit, followed by RSPM, then SPM, and finally Ammonia with the highest permissible limit among the four. This increasing sequence – Lead, RSPM, SPM, Ammonia – corresponds exactly to (C), (B), (A), (D), Option 4.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – (A), (B), (C), (D) – places SPM and RSPM before Lead, reversing the correct low-to-high toxicity-based ordering.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – (B), (A), (C), (D) – places RSPM and SPM before Lead, again reversing the correct order.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – (C), (D), (B), (A) – correctly starts with Lead but wrongly places Ammonia second and SPM last, out of sequence.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – (C), (B), (A), (D) – correctly orders Lead $&lt;$ RSPM $&lt;$ SPM $&lt;$ Ammonia.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>CPCB 24-hr residential limits (increasing): Lead $&lt;$ RSPM $&lt;$ SPM $&lt;$ Ammonia.</li><li>Lead’s limit is extremely tight due to its high toxicity even in trace amounts.</li><li>Mnemonic: <b>L-R-S-A</b> (Lead, RSPM, SPM, Ammonia) – increasing permissible limits.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_e',no:39,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> Statement I: A nuclear power reactor is a system designed to sustain a fission chain reaction and extract useful energy.<br> statement II: Light water reactors use ordinary water as the moderator.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true.','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are false.','C) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.','D) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">A <b>nuclear power reactor</b> is precisely a system engineered to sustain a controlled <b>nuclear fission chain reaction</b> and harness the released energy (typically to generate heat for electricity production). <b>Light Water Reactors (LWRs)</b> – the most common reactor type worldwide (including Pressurised Water Reactors and Boiling Water Reactors) – use ordinary (“light”) water both as a <b>coolant</b> and as the <b>neutron moderator</b>, which slows fast neutrons to sustain the chain reaction efficiently.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Statement I defines a nuclear power reactor; Statement II describes the moderator used in light water reactors.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Verify the truth of each statement.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Recall standard nuclear-engineering definitions of reactors and moderators.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Evaluate Statement I:</b> This is the textbook definition of a nuclear power reactor – sustaining fission and extracting useful energy – <b>true</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Evaluate Statement II:</b> Light Water Reactors are specifically named for their use of ordinary (light) water, which serves as both coolant and moderator – <b>true</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Combine results:</b> Both statements accurately describe standard nuclear reactor concepts.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Option 1 – Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements are accurate, standard descriptions from nuclear engineering: a nuclear power reactor is indeed defined by its ability to sustain a controlled fission chain reaction for useful energy extraction, and light water reactors are indeed named for and defined by their use of ordinary water as the neutron moderator. This makes Option 1 correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Both statements correctly describe standard nuclear reactor concepts.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Both false is incorrect, as both statements are accurate, standard definitions.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Statement I true, II false – incorrect because Statement II is also accurate; light water reactors do use ordinary water as moderator.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Statement I false, II true – incorrect because Statement I is also an accurate definition of a nuclear power reactor.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Nuclear power reactor: sustains a fission chain reaction, extracts useful energy.</li><li>Light Water Reactor (LWR): uses ordinary water as both coolant and moderator.</li><li>LWRs (PWRs and BWRs) are the most widely used reactor type globally.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_e',no:40,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> Statement I: Composting is aerobic degradation of organic materials under controlled conditions, yielding a marketable manure.<br> Statement II: In such composts, concentrations of key nutrients such as nitrogen, phosphorous and potassium are sufficient enough to compete with commercial fertilizers.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true.','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are false.','C) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.','D) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Composting</b> is correctly defined as the <b>aerobic</b> (oxygen-requiring) biological degradation of organic waste under controlled conditions, producing a stable, marketable manure/soil conditioner. However, compost’s <b>NPK (nitrogen-phosphorus-potassium)</b> nutrient concentrations are typically <b>much lower and less standardised</b> than those in chemically formulated commercial fertilizers, which are engineered to deliver precise, high-concentration nutrient doses – so compost <b>does not</b> genuinely compete with commercial fertilizers on nutrient concentration, even though it offers other soil-health benefits (organic matter, micronutrients, soil structure).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1 – Given information:</b> Statement I defines composting; Statement II claims compost’s NPK levels rival commercial fertilizers.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2 – Required result:</b> Verify the truth of each statement.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 – Principle used:</b> Recall the standard definition of composting and typical nutrient-concentration comparisons between compost and chemical fertilizers.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4 – Evaluate Statement I:</b> Composting is indeed defined as controlled aerobic degradation of organic matter, producing a marketable manure – <b>true</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5 – Evaluate Statement II:</b> Compost’s NPK content is generally low and variable compared to the concentrated, standardised nutrient content of commercial chemical fertilizers – so it does <i>not</i> genuinely “compete with” commercial fertilizers on nutrient concentration – <b>false</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 – Combine results:</b> Statement I is true, Statement II is false.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7 – Correct option:</b> Option 3 – Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Composting is accurately described in Statement I as an aerobic, controlled degradation process producing usable manure. However, Statement II overstates compost’s nutrient value – its NPK concentrations are typically modest and cannot match the precisely engineered, high-concentration nutrient profiles of commercial fertilizers – making Statement II false and Option 3 the correct answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Both true is incorrect since Statement II overstates compost’s nutrient competitiveness with commercial fertilizers.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Both false is incorrect since Statement I is an accurate definition of composting.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Statement I true, Statement II false – correctly distinguishes the accurate process definition from the overstated nutrient claim.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Statement I false, Statement II true – reverses the correct truth values of both statements.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Composting = aerobic degradation of organic matter under controlled conditions $\\to$ marketable manure.</li><li>Compost’s NPK levels are generally <i>lower</i> than commercial fertilizers – it does not “compete” on nutrient concentration.</li><li>Compost’s real value: organic matter, micronutrients, soil structure – not headline NPK content.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 08 Dec 2023 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_d',no:36,q:'In which one of the following Conference of Parties (COP) Kyoto protocol was adopted?',opts:['A) COP - 1','B) COP - 3','C) COP - 7','D) COP - 10'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> The <b>Kyoto Protocol</b> – a landmark international treaty committing industrialised nations to binding greenhouse-gas emission-reduction targets – was adopted at the <b>Third Conference of the Parties (COP-3)</b> to the UNFCCC, held in Kyoto, Japan, in <b>December 1997</b> (and entered into force in 2005).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> The question asks at which numbered COP session the Kyoto Protocol was adopted.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Identify the correct COP number from the four given options.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Direct historical recall of major UNFCCC COP milestones.</li><li><b>Step 4–6 (Recall and match):</b> COP-1 (1995, Berlin) produced the Berlin Mandate, not the Kyoto Protocol; COP-3 (1997, Kyoto) is where the Kyoto Protocol was formally adopted, giving the treaty its name; COP-7 (2001, Marrakesh) produced the Marrakesh Accords (implementation rules); COP-10 (2004, Buenos Aires) was a later routine session, not an adoption milestone.</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> COP-3 matches <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The Kyoto Protocol is named directly after the city (Kyoto, Japan) that hosted the COP session at which it was adopted – COP-3 in December 1997 – making the city name itself a strong memory anchor for the correct COP number.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — COP-1 – the first COP (Berlin, 1995) predates the Kyoto Protocol and produced the Berlin Mandate, not Kyoto; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — COP-3 – correctly identifies the Kyoto (1997) session at which the protocol was adopted; correct.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — COP-7 – Marrakesh (2001), which finalised <i>implementation</i> rules for Kyoto, not its original adoption; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — COP-10 – Buenos Aires (2004), a later, routine COP session unrelated to Kyoto’s adoption; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">The treaty’s very name (“Kyoto” Protocol) directly identifies the host city of COP-3, the session at which it was formally adopted in December 1997 – an unambiguous historical fact confirming Option 2.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Name-to-number anchor: since the protocol is literally named after its host city, simply recalling “Kyoto $=$ 1997 $=$ third major COP meeting” bypasses the need to separately memorise a numeric COP index at all.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Kyoto Protocol adopted at COP-3, Kyoto, Japan, December 1997.</li><li>COP-1 (Berlin, 1995): Berlin Mandate. COP-7 (Marrakesh, 2001): Marrakesh Accords.</li><li>The treaty’s name directly signals its host city and hence its COP number.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>KYOTO = 3 syllables = COP-3.</b> A light mnemonic: “Ky-o-to” has three syllables, echoing COP-<b>3</b> – a quick memory hook for the exact session number.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Confusing the Kyoto Protocol’s adoption COP (COP-3, 1997) with the COP at which its detailed implementation rules were finalised (COP-7, Marrakesh, 2001) – these are two distinct milestones in the same treaty’s history.</div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_d',no:37,q:'If a water body is healthy and supports no significant biological activity, then its condition is called as',opts:['A) Hypotrophic','B) Mesotrophic','C) Eutrophic','D) Oligotrophic'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> Water bodies are classified along a trophic (nutrient-richness) spectrum: <b>Oligotrophic</b> water bodies are nutrient-poor, very clear, and support <i>minimal</i> biological/algal activity (yet are typically “healthy” in the sense of high dissolved oxygen and low pollution); <b>Mesotrophic</b> water bodies have moderate nutrients and biological activity; <b>Eutrophic</b> water bodies are nutrient-rich (often from pollution/runoff) and support excessive biological activity (e.g. algal blooms), frequently leading to oxygen depletion. “Hypotrophic” is not a standard trophic-status term used in this classification.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> The water body described is healthy but supports <i>no significant</i> biological activity.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Name the correct trophic-status term.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Match “low nutrient/low biological activity” against the standard oligotrophic-mesotrophic-eutrophic spectrum.</li><li><b>Step 4–6 (Match against each option):</b> “Hypotrophic” is not a term used in standard limnological classification; “Mesotrophic” implies moderate (not minimal) biological activity; “Eutrophic” implies excessive (not minimal) biological activity, typically associated with pollution; “Oligotrophic” precisely describes low-nutrient, low-biological-activity, typically clear and healthy water bodies.</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> Oligotrophic matches <b>Option 4</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The oligotrophic-mesotrophic-eutrophic spectrum classifies water bodies by nutrient (especially nitrogen and phosphorus) concentration, which directly drives biological productivity (algae, plankton, plant growth). At the low end, oligotrophic lakes/water bodies (e.g. many high-altitude, glacially fed lakes) are nutrient-poor and support minimal biological growth while remaining clear and well-oxygenated – consistent with the “healthy...no significant biological activity” description in the question.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Hypotrophic – not a standard term in the oligotrophic/mesotrophic/eutrophic classification scheme; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Mesotrophic – describes an intermediate nutrient/biological-activity level, not the minimal-activity condition described; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — Eutrophic – describes high-nutrient water bodies with excessive (often problematic) biological activity, the opposite of what is described; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — Oligotrophic – correctly describes a nutrient-poor, minimally biologically active, typically healthy and clear water body; correct.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">Since the standard three-tier classification (oligotrophic $&lt;$ mesotrophic $&lt;$ eutrophic, by nutrient/productivity level) places minimal biological activity uniquely at the oligotrophic end, and the fourth option (“hypotrophic”) is not a recognised term in this scheme at all, Option 4 is the only valid match.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Prefix-meaning method: “Oligo-” means “few/little” (as in “oligopoly” = few sellers) – directly signalling <i>few</i> nutrients and <i>little</i> biological activity; “Eu-” means “good/well” or, in this ecological context, “well-nourished” (excessive nutrients) – the opposite condition; recognising these Greek prefixes alone resolves the question.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Oligotrophic = nutrient-poor, minimal biological activity, typically clear and healthy.</li><li>Mesotrophic = moderate nutrients/activity; Eutrophic = nutrient-rich, excessive activity (often polluted).</li><li>“Hypotrophic” is not part of this standard classification.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>OLIGO = FEW.</b> Just as an “oligarchy” is rule by a <i>few</i>, an oligotrophic lake has <i>few</i> nutrients and correspondingly little biological growth.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Assuming “healthy” water automatically means “highly productive/active” – in trophic-status terminology, a healthy, unpolluted, clear water body is typically <i>oligotrophic</i> (low activity), while high biological activity (eutrophic) is usually a sign of nutrient pollution, not health.</div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_d',no:38,q:'To qualify as hazardous waste, a waste must have the following characteristics:<br><br>A. Ignitability B. Corrosivity C. Reactivity D. Toxicity E. Radioactivity<br><br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, C and D only','B) C, D and E only','C) A, B, C and E only','D) A, B, C, D and E'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> As per the answer key for this NTA question, a waste stream is classified/qualified as <b>hazardous</b> when it exhibits <i>any one or more</i> of the following characteristics: <b>Ignitability</b> (easily catches fire), <b>Corrosivity</b> (strongly acidic/basic, corrodes materials/tissue), <b>Reactivity</b> (unstable, reacts violently/explosively), <b>Toxicity</b> (harmful/poisonous if ingested/absorbed), and <b>Radioactivity</b> (emits harmful ionising radiation) – all five properties are recognised, in this examination context, as qualifying characteristics of hazardous waste.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> Five candidate characteristics (A–E) potentially qualifying a waste as hazardous.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Identify the complete, correct set of qualifying characteristics per the exam’s key.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Match each candidate characteristic against the standard, syllabus-referenced list of hazardous-waste properties (ignitability, corrosivity, reactivity, toxicity) and this paper’s officially keyed inclusion of radioactivity as a fifth qualifying property.</li><li><b>Step 4–6 (Evaluate all five):</b> Ignitability, Corrosivity, Reactivity and Toxicity form the commonly cited “ICRT” core set of hazardous-waste characteristics; Radioactivity, while sometimes separately regulated, is included as a qualifying hazardous characteristic in this examination’s answer key – all five (A–E) are therefore accepted as correct.</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> All five (A, B, C, D and E) matches <b>Option 4</b>, per the officially released key.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Hazardous-waste identification frameworks commonly centre on four core characteristics (ignitability, corrosivity, reactivity, toxicity) that make a waste dangerous to handle, store, or dispose of improperly. This examination’s key additionally credits radioactivity as a fifth qualifying characteristic, reflecting a broader, syllabus-consistent view of “hazardous” that also covers radioactive/nuclear waste streams alongside the four chemical-physical hazard categories.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — A, C and D only – omits Corrosivity and Radioactivity, both of which are credited as qualifying characteristics in this exam’s key; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — C, D and E only – omits Ignitability and Corrosivity, both standard qualifying characteristics; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — A, B, C and E only – omits Toxicity, one of the most fundamental hazardous-waste characteristics; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — A, B, C, D and E – includes every listed characteristic (ignitability, corrosivity, reactivity, toxicity, radioactivity) as qualifying, matching the officially released answer key; correct.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">Since each of the five listed properties independently represents a recognised way in which a waste stream can pose a danger to health, safety, or the environment, and the officially released NTA/Human Peritus key credits Option 4, all five together form the complete, correct answer set for this specific question.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Comprehensive-hazard-category cross-check: group the five properties by hazard <i>type</i> – fire hazard (Ignitability), chemical-burn hazard (Corrosivity), instability hazard (Reactivity), poisoning hazard (Toxicity), and radiation hazard (Radioactivity) – observing that each represents a genuinely distinct and non-overlapping category of danger supports treating all five as valid, independent qualifying characteristics rather than assuming any one is redundant or excludable.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Core “ICRT” hazardous-waste characteristics: Ignitability, Corrosivity, Reactivity, Toxicity.</li><li>This examination’s key additionally credits Radioactivity as a fifth qualifying characteristic.</li><li>Correct answer per the key: all five (A, B, C, D and E).</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Exam tip</div><div class="p1x-b">When a “choose the correct combination” question lists a well-known core set (like ICRT) <i>plus</i> one additional, plausible-sounding property, do not automatically exclude the extra property – verify against the specific answer key/syllabus context, as inclusion can vary by source.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Automatically excluding Radioactivity on the assumption that only the classical four-property (ICRT) hazardous-waste framework applies – always match your answer to the specific exam’s officially released key rather than a single remembered textbook list, since coverage can legitimately vary across sources.</div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_d',no:39,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement (I):</b> Acid rain occurs within, and downwind of areas of major industrial emissions of sulfur dioxide and oxides of nitrogen.<br> <b>Statement (II):</b> Long range transport of airborne pollutants can be responsible for acid rain in areas far off from industrial imissions.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are false.','C) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.','D) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> <b>Acid rain</b> forms when sulfur dioxide (SO<sub>2</sub>) and nitrogen oxides (NO<sub>x</sub>) released mainly from industrial/fossil-fuel combustion react with atmospheric water vapour to form sulfuric and nitric acids. Because these gaseous precursors can remain airborne and travel with prevailing winds for hundreds of kilometres before precipitating, acid rain affects both the immediate/downwind vicinity of emission sources <i>and</i> distant regions reached via long-range atmospheric transport – a well-documented, transboundary pollution phenomenon.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> Statement I describes acid rain occurring within and downwind of major emission sources; Statement II describes long-range transport causing acid rain far from the original emission source.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Evaluate the truth of each statement.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Recall the standard atmospheric-chemistry account of acid rain formation and dispersion.</li><li><b>Step 4 (Evaluate Statement I):</b> SO<sub>2</sub>/NO<sub>x</sub> emissions do indeed cause acid rain both at and downwind of major industrial source regions – a well-established, textbook-accurate claim – <b>True</b>.</li><li><b>Step 5 (Evaluate Statement II):</b> Long-range atmospheric transport of these same pollutants is a documented cause of acid rain occurring far from their original industrial source (e.g. transboundary acid rain disputes between neighbouring countries/regions) – <b>True</b>.</li><li><b>Step 6 (Combine):</b> Both statements describe complementary, non-contradictory aspects (near-source vs. far-field) of the same acid-rain phenomenon.</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> Both true matches <b>Option 1</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Acid rain is a classic example of how air pollution does not respect political or geographic boundaries: emissions from a smokestack can cause acidification of soils and water bodies both nearby (Statement I) and, when carried by prevailing winds over long distances before the pollutants react and precipitate out, in regions far removed from any local industrial activity (Statement II) – both effects are well documented and are not mutually exclusive.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — both true – accurately reflects both the local/downwind and long-range transboundary causes of acid rain; correct.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — both false – wrongly rejects two well-established, textbook-accurate claims about acid rain’s causation; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — I true, II false – wrongly denies the well-documented long-range/transboundary transport mechanism of acid rain; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — I false, II true – wrongly denies the basic local/downwind mechanism of acid rain near major emission sources; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">Both the near-field (local/downwind) and far-field (long-range transport) causation mechanisms of acid rain are independently well-supported by atmospheric science and are complementary rather than contradictory, jointly confirming Option 1.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Real-world case-study recall: the historical acid rain disputes between the United States and Canada, and separately within Europe (e.g. Scandinavian lakes acidified by emissions originating in other countries), are commonly cited textbook examples that simultaneously verify both the local/downwind effect and the long-range transboundary effect described in the two statements.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Acid rain forms from SO<sub>2</sub>/NO<sub>x</sub> emissions reacting with atmospheric moisture.</li><li>It affects areas within and downwind of emission sources (local effect) AND, via long-range transport, areas far from the source (transboundary effect).</li><li>Both effects are well-documented and simultaneously true.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Exam tip</div><div class="p1x-b">For environment-science statement pairs describing a “near effect” and a “far/long-range effect” of the same pollutant, both are very often simultaneously true – pollution dispersion mechanisms are rarely mutually exclusive between local and long-range scales.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Assuming that because Statement II describes a <i>far-off</i> effect, it must somehow contradict Statement I’s <i>local</i> effect – both near-source and far-field impacts of airborne pollutants are well-documented and can, and do, occur simultaneously from the same emission source.</div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_d',no:40,q:'‘Life below water’ pertains to which of the SDGs?',opts:['A) SDG 13','B) SDG 14','C) SDG 15','D) SDG 12'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Concept required:</b> Among the UN’s 17 <b>Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs, 2015–2030)</b>, <b>SDG 14 (“Life Below Water”)</b> calls for the conservation and sustainable use of oceans, seas, and marine resources. It is closely flanked by SDG 13 (“Climate Action”) and SDG 15 (“Life on Land”), and SDG 12 covers “Responsible Consumption and Production” – all easily confused by number if not memorised precisely.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b>Step 1 (Given information):</b> The question asks which SDG number corresponds to “Life Below Water”.</li><li><b>Step 2 (Required result):</b> Identify the correct SDG number.</li><li><b>Step 3 (Principle used):</b> Direct recall of the official UN SDG numbering (1 through 17).</li><li><b>Step 4–6 (Recall and match):</b> SDG 13 is “Climate Action”; SDG 15 is “Life on Land” (terrestrial ecosystems); SDG 12 is “Responsible Consumption and Production”; SDG 14 is specifically “Life Below Water” (oceans/marine ecosystems).</li><li><b>Step 7 (Correct option):</b> SDG 14 matches <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Conceptual explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The 17 SDGs are numbered in a fixed, official sequence adopted by the UN General Assembly in 2015; “Life Below Water” (marine conservation, SDG 14) and “Life on Land” (terrestrial biodiversity, SDG 15) are consecutive, thematically paired goals – a frequent source of number confusion that this question directly tests.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Explanation of every option &amp; why the others are incorrect</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — SDG 13 – this is “Climate Action”, not “Life Below Water”; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> — SDG 14 – correctly identifies “Life Below Water” as the fourteenth Sustainable Development Goal; correct.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — SDG 15 – this is “Life on Land” (terrestrial ecosystems), the water-goal’s paired but distinct land counterpart; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> — SDG 12 – this is “Responsible Consumption and Production”, unrelated to marine life specifically; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Justification of the correct answer</div><div class="p1x-b">The UN’s official SDG list fixes “Life Below Water” unambiguously at position 14, immediately preceding “Life on Land” (SDG 15) – a fixed factual sequence that directly confirms Option 2 and rules out all three distractors.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Alternative solution method</div><div class="p1x-b">Sequential-pairing recall: memorise SDG 13–15 as a themed mini-block – “13 Climate, 14 Water (Below), 15 Land (On)” – reciting this three-item micro-sequence in order instantly resolves any confusion between the closely numbered and thematically related goals 13, 14 and 15.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>SDG 14 = Life Below Water (oceans, seas, marine resources).</li><li>SDG 13 = Climate Action; SDG 15 = Life on Land; SDG 12 = Responsible Consumption and Production.</li><li>Memorise SDGs 13–15 together as a themed block to avoid number confusion.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>“Below” comes before “On”: 14 before 15.</b> Water is literally <i>below</i> land – and numerically, “Life Below Water” (14) comes directly <i>before</i> “Life on Land” (15) in the SDG list, mirroring their real-world spatial relationship.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Confusing SDG 14 (Life Below Water) with SDG 15 (Life on Land) simply because both goals concern “life”/biodiversity and sit next to each other numerically – always pair the specific keyword (“Below Water” vs. “on Land”) with its exact number.</div></div>'},
  // ── 08 Dec 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_c',no:36,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> The total solids (dissolved and suspended) in a waste water sample are the materials left after water has evaporated from the sample.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> The dissolved solids fraction usually includes colloidal particles.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate answer</i> from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">In standard wastewater engineering (Total Solids analysis), <b>Total Solids (TS)</b> is operationally defined as the residue remaining after a wastewater sample is evaporated and dried – this includes both suspended and dissolved matter. TS is analytically separated into <b>Suspended Solids</b> (retained on a standard filter) and <b>Total Dissolved Solids (TDS)</b> (passing through the filter). Because standard laboratory filters used in this test have a pore size around 1–2 micrometres, sub-micron <b>colloidal particles</b> (roughly 0.001–1 micrometre) mostly pass through the filter along with the truly dissolved matter, so they are conventionally grouped, in this operational classification, within the <b>dissolved solids</b> fraction.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Evaluate Statement I: Total solids are defined, by standard method, as the dry residue left after evaporating water from the sample – this matches the textbook definition exactly – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Evaluate Statement II: because colloidal particles are small enough to pass through the standard glass-fibre filter used to separate suspended from dissolved solids, they are conventionally counted as part of the dissolved solids fraction in wastewater analysis – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Combine the findings: both statements are accurate descriptions of standard wastewater-solids terminology.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Correct option: Option (1).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Total solids is correctly defined as the evaporation residue, and the operational (filter-based) definition of dissolved solids conventionally captures colloidal particles as well (since they pass through the standard filter), so both statements are correct, confirming Option (1).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – both statements match the standard operational definitions used in wastewater-solids analysis.</li><li><b>Option (2)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – both statements are, in fact, accurate.</li><li><b>Option (3)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly treats Statement II as false; the filter-based operational method does place colloids within the dissolved-solids fraction.</li><li><b>Option (4)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly treats Statement I as false; the evaporation-residue definition of total solids is standard and correct.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Total Solids = residue left after evaporating a wastewater sample to dryness.</li><li>Total Solids = Suspended Solids (retained on filter) + Dissolved Solids (passes through filter).</li><li>Colloidal particles, being sub-micron, typically pass the standard filter and are grouped with dissolved solids in this operational classification.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_c',no:37,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement (I):</b> When a hazardous waste has either high or low pH value, it must be treated for neutralization before discharge.<br> <b>Statement (II):</b> Sulfuric acid and Hydrochloric acids are added to hazardous waste with low pH value to neutralise it.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate answer</i> from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Corrosivity</b> (extreme high or low pH) is one of the standard characteristics that classifies a waste as hazardous, and such waste requires <b>neutralisation</b> before safe discharge. Neutralisation follows basic acid-base chemistry: an <b>acidic</b> (low pH) waste is neutralised by adding a <b>base</b> (e.g., lime, sodium hydroxide), and an <b>alkaline</b> (high pH) waste is neutralised by adding an <b>acid</b>. Adding <i>more acid</i> (such as sulfuric or hydrochloric acid) to an already low-pH (acidic) waste would only make the waste more acidic, not neutral.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Evaluate Statement I: extreme pH (either too high or too low) is a recognised hazardous-waste characteristic (corrosivity) requiring neutralisation before discharge – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Evaluate Statement II: sulfuric acid and hydrochloric acid are themselves strong <i>acids</i>; adding them to waste that is <i>already</i> acidic (low pH) would lower the pH further, not neutralise it – the correct treatment for low-pH waste is to add a <i>base</i>, not more acid – <b>False</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Combine the findings: Statement I is correct, Statement II is incorrect.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Correct option: Option (3).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">While it is true that extreme-pH hazardous waste requires neutralisation (Statement I), the specific treatment described in Statement II is chemically backwards: adding strong acids to already-acidic (low-pH) waste would worsen, not neutralise, the corrosivity, so Statement II is false, confirming Option (3).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Statement II describes a chemically incorrect neutralisation procedure.</li><li><b>Option (2)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Statement I is a correct, standard hazardous-waste-management principle.</li><li><b>Option (3)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – correctly separates the true Statement I from the chemically incorrect Statement II.</li><li><b>Option (4)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – reverses the true pattern; it is Statement II, not Statement I, that is incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Corrosivity (high or low pH) is a recognised characteristic of hazardous waste, requiring neutralisation before discharge.</li><li>Low-pH (acidic) waste is neutralised by adding a <b>base</b> (e.g., lime); high-pH (alkaline) waste is neutralised by adding an <b>acid</b>.</li><li>Adding an acid (sulfuric/hydrochloric) to already-acidic waste is chemically the wrong direction – a classic “opposite reagent” trap.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_c',no:38,q:'Which of the following gases were covered under Kyoto Protocol?<br> (A) Carbon dioxide (CO$_2$)<br> (B) Methane (CH$_4$)<br> (C) Nitrogen dioxide (NO$_2$)<br> (D) Nitrous oxide (N$_2$O)<br> (E) Sulfur dioxide (SO$_2$)<br> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (B) and (D) Only','B) (A), (B) and (C) Only','C) (B), (C) and (D) Only','D) (C), (D) and (E) Only'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The Kyoto Protocol (1997) targets a specific “basket” of <b>six</b> greenhouse gases: Carbon dioxide (CO$_2$), Methane (CH$_4$), Nitrous oxide (N$_2$O), Hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs), Perfluorocarbons (PFCs), and Sulfur hexafluoride (SF$_6$). Note carefully that the protocol covers <b>Nitrous oxide (N$_2$O)</b>, not Nitrogen dioxide (NO$_2$), and <b>Sulfur hexafluoride (SF$_6$)</b>, not Sulfur dioxide (SO$_2$) – NO$_2$ and SO$_2$ are conventional <i>air pollutants</i> regulated under separate air-quality frameworks, not part of the Kyoto GHG basket.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Recall the Kyoto Protocol’s basket of six greenhouse gases: CO$_2$, CH$_4$, N$_2$O, HFCs, PFCs, SF$_6$.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Check (A) Carbon dioxide (CO$_2$): explicitly part of the Kyoto basket – <b>include</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Check (B) Methane (CH$_4$): explicitly part of the Kyoto basket – <b>include</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Check (C) Nitrogen dioxide (NO$_2$): <i>not</i> part of the Kyoto basket (the basket covers Nitrous oxide, N$_2$O, a chemically different gas) – <b>exclude</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Check (D) Nitrous oxide (N$_2$O): explicitly part of the Kyoto basket – <b>include</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> Check (E) Sulfur dioxide (SO$_2$): <i>not</i> part of the Kyoto basket (the basket covers Sulfur hexafluoride, SF$_6$, a chemically different gas) – <b>exclude</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> Collect the valid items: (A), (B) and (D) Only.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">8.</span> Correct option: Option (1).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Only CO$_2$, CH$_4$ and N$_2$O among the five listed gases actually belong to the Kyoto Protocol’s basket of six GHGs; NO$_2$ and SO$_2$ are deliberately similar-looking but chemically distinct gases that are <i>not</i> part of the Kyoto basket, confirming Option (1).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – correctly identifies CO$_2$, CH$_4$ and N$_2$O as the three Kyoto-covered gases among those listed.</li><li><b>Option (2)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly includes Nitrogen dioxide (NO$_2$), which is not part of the Kyoto basket, and omits Nitrous oxide (N$_2$O), which is.</li><li><b>Option (3)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly includes NO$_2$ and drops CO$_2$, one of the most prominent Kyoto-covered gases.</li><li><b>Option (4)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – wrongly includes NO$_2$ and SO$_2$, neither of which belongs to the Kyoto basket, while omitting CO$_2$ and CH$_4$.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Kyoto Protocol’s six GHGs: CO$_2$, CH$_4$, N$_2$O, HFCs, PFCs, SF$_6$.</li><li>Watch for the deliberate near-miss substitutions: NO$_2$ (not covered) vs. N$_2$O (covered); SO$_2$ (not covered) vs. SF$_6$ (covered).</li><li>NO$_2$ and SO$_2$ are common air pollutants (acid rain, smog precursors) but are outside the Kyoto GHG basket.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_c',no:39,q:'Temperature inversion in the atmosphere mainly causes?',opts:['A) Dust storm','B) Dispersion of air pollutants','C) Accumulation of air pollutants','D) Ozone depletion'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Normally, air temperature decreases with altitude, allowing warm air near the ground to rise, carrying pollutants upward and away (vertical mixing/dispersion). A <b>temperature inversion</b> reverses this pattern: a layer of warmer air sits <i>above</i> a layer of cooler air near the surface, acting like a “lid” that suppresses vertical air movement. This traps pollutants close to the ground instead of allowing them to disperse.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Recall the normal atmospheric temperature profile: temperature falls with height, so warm surface air rises and disperses pollutants.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Recall what a temperature inversion changes: a warmer layer sits above a cooler surface layer, halting the normal upward convective movement of air.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Deduce the consequence: with vertical mixing suppressed, pollutants emitted near the ground cannot rise and disperse, so they build up (accumulate) in the near-surface air layer.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Eliminate Dust storm (caused by strong winds, not inversion), Dispersion of pollutants (the opposite effect), and Ozone depletion (linked to stratospheric chemical reactions with CFCs, not to surface temperature inversions).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Correct option: Option (3), Accumulation of air pollutants.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">By suppressing the normal upward convective mixing of air, a temperature inversion traps pollutants near the ground rather than letting them disperse – this trapping/build-up is precisely “accumulation,” confirming Option (3).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) Dust storm</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – dust storms arise from strong surface winds over dry/loose soil, unrelated to a temperature inversion.</li><li><b>Option (2) Dispersion of air pollutants</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – this is exactly the <i>opposite</i> of what an inversion causes; inversions suppress, not promote, dispersion.</li><li><b>Option (3) Accumulation of air pollutants</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – matches the trapping effect of suppressed vertical mixing under an inversion.</li><li><b>Option (4) Ozone depletion</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – caused primarily by stratospheric reactions involving ozone-depleting substances (e.g., CFCs), not by surface-level temperature inversions.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Normal profile: temperature falls with height $\\rightarrow$ pollutants disperse upward.</li><li>Inversion: warm air above cool air $\\rightarrow$ vertical mixing suppressed $\\rightarrow$ pollutants accumulate near ground.</li><li>Winter mornings and valleys are classic settings where temperature inversions worsen local air quality (e.g., smog build-up).</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_c',no:40,q:'Arrange the following in increasing order of DDT concentration among them as a result of bioaccumulation/accumulation:<br> (A) Fish<br> (B) Water body<br> (C) Fish eating birds<br> (D) Zooplanklons<br> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (B), (C), (D)','B) (B), (D), (A), (C)','C) (C), (D), (A), (B)','D) (B), (A), (C), (D)'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Biomagnification</b> is the progressive increase in concentration of a persistent, fat-soluble pollutant (such as DDT) at each successive trophic level of a food chain, because predators consume and retain the accumulated pollutant load of many prey organisms. The classic aquatic DDT food chain runs: Water (very low, dilute concentration) $\\to$ Zooplankton (primary consumers, first uptake and concentration) $\\to$ Fish (secondary/tertiary consumers, eating many zooplankton) $\\to$ Fish-eating birds (apex predators, eating many fish, so DDT concentration is highest here).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Identify the trophic position of each item: Water body (abiotic source, lowest concentration) $\\to$ Zooplankton (primary consumers) $\\to$ Fish (consume many zooplankton) $\\to$ Fish-eating birds (apex predators, consume many fish).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Apply the biomagnification principle: DDT concentration increases at each higher trophic level.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Arrange in increasing order of concentration: Water body (B) $\\to$ Zooplankton (D) $\\to$ Fish (A) $\\to$ Fish-eating birds (C).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Match this order to the given options: (B), (D), (A), (C).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Correct option: Option (2).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">DDT is fat-soluble and persistent, so it becomes progressively more concentrated as it moves up the aquatic food chain from water, to zooplankton, to fish, to the fish-eating birds at the top – giving the increasing-concentration order (B), (D), (A), (C), which is Option (2).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – places Fish (A) before Water body (B), reversing the lowest starting point of the food chain.</li><li><b>Option (2)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – correctly follows the trophic sequence water $\\to$ zooplankton $\\to$ fish $\\to$ fish-eating birds.</li><li><b>Option (3)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – places the apex predator, Fish-eating birds (C), first (as if lowest concentration), a complete reversal of the biomagnification principle.</li><li><b>Option (4)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – swaps the positions of Fish (A) and Zooplankton (D), placing Fish below Zooplankton in the trophic order.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Biomagnification: pollutant concentration increases at each higher trophic level.</li><li>Classic DDT chain: Water $&lt;$ Zooplankton $&lt;$ Fish $&lt;$ Fish-eating birds.</li><li>Apex predators (top of the food chain) always carry the highest concentration of a biomagnifying pollutant.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 07 Dec 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_b',no:36,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> Statement (I): Exposure to mercury can be either through food chain or in the work place.<br> Statement (II): Poisoning by inorganic mercury (particularly vapours) results in damage to the central nervous system.<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true.','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are false.','C) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.','D) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Mercury as an environmental/occupational pollutant.</b> Mercury exposure occurs via two principal routes: dietary (bioaccumulation in the food chain, e.g., contaminated fish – as tragically demonstrated in the Minamata disease episode) and occupational (workplace exposure in mining, chlor-alkali plants, thermometer/battery manufacturing). Inorganic mercury vapour is neurotoxic; chronic inhalation damages the central nervous system, causing tremors, memory loss and cognitive impairment.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements are scientifically accurate descriptions of mercury’s exposure pathways and toxicological effects, so Option (A) ‘Both true’ is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> Mercury exposure via food chain/workplace (I) and CNS damage from inorganic mercury vapour poisoning (II) are both well-established environmental-health facts.</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – both statements are, in fact, true.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – Statement (II) is also true, not false.</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – Statement (I) is also true, not false.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Mercury exposure routes: food chain (bioaccumulation, e.g., fish) and occupational/workplace exposure.</li><li>Inorganic mercury vapour is neurotoxic – damages the central nervous system.</li><li>Minamata disease (Japan) is the classic case study of methylmercury poisoning via the food chain – often cross-referenced in Unit IX questions.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_b',no:37,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> Statement (I): Kyoto protocol only binds developed countries and places heavier burden on them.<br> Statement (II): Kyoto protocol has nothing to do with United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC).<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true.','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are false.','C) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.','D) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Kyoto Protocol.</b> Adopted in 1997 under the <i>UNFCCC</i>, the Kyoto Protocol operationalises the Convention by setting legally binding emission-reduction targets <i>only</i> for developed (‘Annex I’) countries, based on the principle of Common But Differentiated Responsibilities (CBDR) – recognising that developed nations bear greater historical responsibility for emissions. It is therefore intrinsically and institutionally linked to the UNFCCC, not separate from it.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Statement (I) is a correct description of the Kyoto Protocol’s differentiated-burden design. Statement (II) is false, because the Kyoto Protocol is in fact a protocol <i>under</i> the UNFCCC framework, not unrelated to it. Hence Option (C) ‘I true, II false’ is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – Statement (II) is false.</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – Statement (I) is true.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> The Protocol does bind only developed countries with a heavier burden (true), but it is very much linked to (an instrument under) the UNFCCC, not unrelated to it (so II is false).</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – it is Statement (I), not (II), that is correct.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Kyoto Protocol (1997) $\\Rightarrow$ adopted under UNFCCC; binding emission targets only for developed (Annex I) countries.</li><li>Principle of Common But Differentiated Responsibilities (CBDR) underlies this developed-country-only obligation.</li><li>Successor agreement: Paris Agreement (2015), which involves commitments from <i>all</i> countries (Nationally Determined Contributions), unlike Kyoto’s developed-country-only scope.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_b',no:38,q:'Match List-I with List-II',opts:['A) A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II','B) A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II','C) A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I','D) A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>pH scale and water categories.</b> Pure/normal water has a neutral pH of $7$. Rainwater is naturally slightly acidic (pH $\\approx 5$–$6$) due to dissolved atmospheric CO$_2$ forming weak carbonic acid. Acid rain (from SO$_2$/NO$_x$ pollution) is more strongly acidic, typically pH $&lt;5$. Ocean/seawater is mildly alkaline, pH $&gt;7$ (typically $\\approx 8.1$), due to dissolved salts and buffering carbonate/bicarbonate systems.<br><br><div class="p1-blk">whitestatupBgGray <div class="p1-tw"><table class="p1-table"><tr><th><b>List I</b></th><th><b>(Water Category)</b></th><th><b>List II</b></th><th><b>(pH Value)</b></th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Normal Water</td><td>I</td><td>$&lt;5$</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Rain water</td><td>II</td><td>$&gt;7$</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>Acid Rain Water</td><td>III</td><td>$5$–$6$</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Ocean Water</td><td>IV</td><td>$7$</td></tr></table></div></div></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">A(Normal water, pH 7)$\\to$IV; B(Rainwater, pH 5–6)$\\to$III; C(Acid rain, pH$&lt;5$)$\\to$I; D(Ocean water, pH$&gt;7$)$\\to$II. This matches <b>A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II</b>, Option (B).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – swaps B and C’s pH matches (rainwater wrongly matched to I, acid rain wrongly matched to III).</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> Normal water (neutral, pH 7), rainwater (mildly acidic, pH 5-6), acid rain (strongly acidic, pH$&lt;$5), and ocean water (mildly alkaline, pH$&gt;$7) are all correctly matched.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – swaps C and D’s pH matches (acid rain wrongly matched to II, ocean water wrongly matched to I).</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – an entirely different, inconsistent mapping not matching the known pH ranges of these water types.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Normal/pure water: pH $=7$ (neutral).</li><li>Rainwater: pH $\\approx5$–$6$ (naturally mildly acidic, due to atmospheric CO$_2$).</li><li>Acid rain: pH $&lt;5$ (industrially/vehicular polluted, SO$_2$/NO$_x$ derived).</li><li>Ocean/seawater: pH $&gt;7$ (mildly alkaline, buffered by dissolved carbonates/bicarbonates).</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_b',no:39,q:'Leachates are',opts:['A) Persistent Organic Pollutants','B) Algae found on eutrophic lakes','C) Ozone depleting substances','D) Contaminated liquid draining from landfills.'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Solid Waste Management terminology.</b> <i>Leachate</i> is the liquid that forms when water percolates through solid waste in a landfill, dissolving and carrying with it contaminants (organic matter, heavy metals, dissolved solids) – it is a major source of groundwater and soil contamination if not properly contained/treated.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">By definition, <b>leachate</b> is the contaminated liquid that drains/percolates out of a landfill (or other solid-waste site), matching Option (D).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Persistent Organic Pollutants (POPs) are a distinct category of long-lived, bio-accumulative toxic chemicals (e.g., DDT, PCBs) – not the definition of leachate.</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Algae found on eutrophic (nutrient-enriched) lakes are unrelated to landfill drainage; this describes an <i>algal bloom</i> phenomenon, not leachate.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Ozone-depleting substances (e.g., CFCs) are atmospheric pollutants affecting the stratospheric ozone layer – an entirely different category from landfill leachate.</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> Leachate is precisely defined as the contaminated liquid draining from a landfill/waste-disposal site.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Leachate = contaminated liquid draining from a landfill, formed by water percolating through waste.</li><li>Distinguish leachate (liquid waste-drainage contamination) from POPs (persistent toxic organic chemicals) and ODS (ozone-depleting substances, e.g., CFCs) – three separate Unit IX pollutant categories.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_b',no:40,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> Statement (I): International Solar Alliance’s vision: ‘Let us together make the sun brighter.’<br> Statement (II): International Solar Alliance’s mission: ‘Every home, how far away, will have a light at home.’<br> In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are true.','B) Both Statement (I) and Statement (II) are false.','C) Statement (I) is true but Statement (II) is false.','D) Statement (I) is false but Statement (II) is true.'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>International Solar Alliance (ISA).</b> Launched jointly by India and France at the 2015 UN Climate Change Conference (COP21, Paris), the ISA is a treaty-based international organisation dedicated to promoting solar energy, especially in sunshine-rich countries. Its officially stated vision and mission are exactly as reproduced in the two statements above.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements accurately quote the ISA’s officially adopted vision (‘Let us together make the sun brighter’) and mission (‘Every home...will have a light at home’) statements, so Option (A) ‘Both true’ is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul p1-desc"><li><b><b>Option (A)</b> – <span class="p1-green">✓ CORRECT</span></b> <b>Correct.</b> Both the vision and mission statements are correctly and verbatim reproduced from ISA’s official framework.</li><li><b><b>Option (B)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – both statements are, in fact, true.</li><li><b><b>Option (C)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – Statement (II) is also true, correctly quoting ISA’s mission.</li><li><b><b>Option (D)</b> – <span class="p1-red">✗ Incorrect</span></b> Incorrect – Statement (I) is also true, correctly quoting ISA’s vision.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>International Solar Alliance (ISA): India-France initiative, launched at COP21 Paris (2015).</li><li>Aim: promote solar-energy deployment, especially among sunshine-abundant developing nations, to reduce fossil-fuel dependence.</li><li>Memorise official taglines of major India-linked international environmental/energy initiatives – frequently and verbatim tested in Unit IX.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 06 Dec 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_a',no:36,q:'Which of the following are true about Producer Gas? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">(A)</span> It is the result of conversion of solid biomass into gaseous fuels.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(B)</span> It is done at lower temperature.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(C)</span> It can be used as fuel in internal combustion engines.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(D)</span> It is produced in the complete absence of oxygen.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(E)</span> Hydrogen and methane present in Producer Gas determine its heating value.</li></ol> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (A), (B) and (C) Only','B) (B) and (D) Only','C) (C), (D) and (E) Only','D) (A), (C) and (E) Only'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Producer gas</b> is generated through <b>gasification</b> – the partial (not complete) combustion of solid biomass or coal in a <b>limited</b>, controlled supply of air at relatively <b>high</b> temperature (typically in the range of several hundred to about 1000°C in a gasifier), converting the solid fuel into a combustible gaseous mixture (chiefly CO, H<sub>2</sub>, CH<sub>4</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>, and CO<sub>2</sub>). Historically, producer gas has been used to power internal combustion engines (including gasifier-fitted vehicles). Its calorific/heating value is determined mainly by its combustible components – hydrogen (H<sub>2</sub>), carbon monoxide (CO), and methane (CH<sub>4</sub>).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Evaluate (A): producer gas is indeed produced by converting solid biomass/coal into gaseous fuel through gasification – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Evaluate (B): gasification to produce producer gas typically occurs at relatively <i>high</i>, not low, temperature – <b>False</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Evaluate (C): producer gas has historically been, and can be, used as a fuel to run internal combustion engines – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Evaluate (D): producer gas is made using a <i>limited/partial</i> supply of air/oxygen (gasification), not a <i>complete absence</i> of oxygen (which would instead describe pyrolysis) – <b>False</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> Evaluate (E): the combustible components hydrogen and methane (along with CO) directly determine the heating/calorific value of producer gas – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> True statements: (A), (C) and (E) – matches option (4).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Producer gas results from partial (limited-air) gasification of biomass at relatively high temperature – so statements claiming “lower temperature” (B) and “complete absence of oxygen” (D) are both factually incorrect descriptions of the gasification process. The remaining three statements – conversion of solid biomass to gas (A), usability as IC-engine fuel (C), and hydrogen/methane determining heating value (E) – are all accurate.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – (A), (B) and (C) Only</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Wrongly includes (B); gasification occurs at high, not low, temperature.</li><li><b>Option (2) – (B) and (D) Only</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Includes only the two false statements (B and D) and omits all three true ones.</li><li><b>Option (3) – (C), (D) and (E) Only</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Wrongly includes (D); gasification uses limited air, not a complete absence of oxygen.</li><li><b>Option (4) – (A), (C) and (E) Only</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: Correctly identifies exactly the three true statements about producer gas.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Common mistake</div><div class="p1x-b">Do not confuse <b>gasification</b> (partial/limited air supply, used to make producer gas) with <b>pyrolysis</b> (complete absence of oxygen, used to make char/bio-oil) – these are distinct thermochemical conversion processes and NTA frequently tests this exact distinction.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Producer gas = solid biomass converted to gaseous fuel via gasification (limited air, relatively high temperature).</li><li>Can be used as fuel in internal combustion engines.</li><li>Heating value determined by combustible components: H<sub>2</sub>, CO, CH<sub>4</sub>.</li><li>Gasification $\\ne$ pyrolysis (complete absence of oxygen).</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_a',no:37,q:'The convention on biological diversity is dedicated to promote sustainable development conceived as a practical tool for translating the principles of:',opts:['A) Agenda 20','B) Agenda 21','C) Agenda 22','D) Agenda 23'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Agenda 21</b> is the comprehensive, non-binding action plan for sustainable development adopted at the 1992 United Nations Conference on Environment and Development (the “Rio Earth Summit”) in Rio de Janeiro. The <b>Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD)</b>, opened for signature at the same Rio Summit, is one of the three related “Rio Conventions” (alongside the UNFCCC on climate change and the UNCCD on desertification) and is explicitly framed as a practical instrument for translating Agenda 21’s sustainable-development principles into action regarding biodiversity conservation.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Recall the historical context: the Convention on Biological Diversity was adopted at the 1992 Rio Earth Summit.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Recall that the Rio Earth Summit’s central action-plan document for sustainable development is named <b>Agenda 21</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Match the CBD’s stated purpose (translating sustainable development principles into a practical tool) to Agenda 21 specifically – “Agenda 20”, “Agenda 22” and “Agenda 23” are not recognised UN sustainable-development documents.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Correct option: (2) Agenda 21.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Agenda 21 is the specific, historically documented action plan associated with the 1992 Rio Earth Summit, and the Convention on Biological Diversity – adopted at that very summit – is explicitly designed to operationalise Agenda 21’s sustainable development principles in the context of biodiversity, making Agenda 21 the only correct, textbook-recognised answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – Agenda 20</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Not a recognised UN sustainable-development document.</li><li><b>Option (2) – Agenda 21</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: The correct, well-documented action plan from the 1992 Rio Earth Summit.</li><li><b>Option (3) – Agenda 22</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Not a recognised UN sustainable-development document.</li><li><b>Option (4) – Agenda 23</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Not a recognised UN sustainable-development document.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Agenda 21 = action plan for sustainable development adopted at the 1992 Rio Earth Summit.</li><li>Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) is one of the three Rio Conventions (with UNFCCC and UNCCD), designed to operationalise Agenda 21’s principles for biodiversity.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_a',no:38,q:'What is the correct sequence of steps followed from initial to last during the water treatment process? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">(A)</span> Coagulation</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(B)</span> Filtration</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(C)</span> Screening</li><li><span class="p1-lab">(D)</span> Sedimentation</li></ol> Choose the <i>correct</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) (D), (C), (B), (A)','B) (D), (C), (A), (B)','C) (C), (D), (A), (B)','D) (C), (D), (B), (A)'],ans:2,bonus:1,exp:'<div class="p1-note">⚠ NTA dropped this question – full marks were awarded to every candidate. Study the concept, not the option.</div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Conventional municipal water treatment generally proceeds through: <b>Screening</b> (removing large debris/solids using screens/bar racks) $\\rightarrow$ <b>Coagulation</b> (adding chemical coagulants to destabilise fine suspended particles) $\\rightarrow$ <b>Flocculation/Sedimentation</b> (allowing coagulated particles to clump and settle out) $\\rightarrow$ <b>Filtration</b> (passing water through filter media to remove remaining fine particles) $\\rightarrow$ Disinfection. Different textbooks and training manuals sometimes present slightly different orderings or combine/split these stages differently, which is very likely why NTA found none of the four listed option sequences to be unambiguously, uniquely correct and chose to drop this question.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Why This Question Was Likely Dropped</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> The standard, most widely taught sequence is Screening $\\rightarrow$ Coagulation $\\rightarrow$ Sedimentation $\\rightarrow$ Filtration – i.e. (C), (A), (D), (B) – but this exact ordering does <b>not</b> appear as any of the four given options.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> All four given options place Screening (C) or Sedimentation (D) inconsistently relative to Coagulation (A), and none matches the standard textbook sequence exactly, creating genuine ambiguity.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Some reference sources describe sedimentation as occurring both before coagulation (pre-sedimentation, to remove grit) and after coagulation/flocculation (to remove flocs) – meaning “Sedimentation” can validly appear at more than one point depending on the specific treatment scheme being referenced, further undermining any single “correct” option among those listed.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Given this genuine textbook-level ambiguity, NTA’s decision to award full marks to all candidates for this item is the academically appropriate resolution.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Rather than memorising any single option here as “correct”, focus on the underlying concept: municipal water treatment always begins with coarse removal (screening) and ends with fine purification (filtration/disinfection), with coagulation and sedimentation working together in between to remove suspended and colloidal impurities. This conceptual sequence is what future NET questions on this topic are likely to test, even though this particular item was dropped.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Q.38 was officially dropped by NTA for this session – full marks awarded to all candidates.</li><li>Conventional conceptual sequence: Screening $\\rightarrow$ Coagulation $\\rightarrow$ Sedimentation $\\rightarrow$ Filtration $\\rightarrow$ Disinfection.</li><li>Do not memorise any single option letter from this question as the confirmed correct answer.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_a',no:39,q:'Given below are two statements: one is labelled as <b>Assertion (A)</b> and the other is labelled as <b>Reason (R)</b>.<br> <b>Assertion (A):</b> DDT bioaccumulates in tissues of many species including humans.<br> <b>Reason (R):</b> DDT is relatively insoluble in water but readily soluble in fats and oils.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)','B) Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is <b>not</b> the correct explanation of (A)','C) (A) is correct but (R) is not correct','D) (A) is not correct but (R) is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>DDT</b> (dichlorodiphenyltrichloroethane) is a <b>lipophilic</b> (fat-soluble) and <b>hydrophobic</b> (water-insoluble) organochlorine pesticide. Because organisms readily store fat-soluble compounds in fatty tissue but cannot easily excrete them through water-based excretory pathways, lipophilic pollutants like DDT accumulate progressively in the fatty tissues of exposed organisms over time – a phenomenon called <b>bioaccumulation</b>, which can further magnify up the food chain (biomagnification).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Evaluate Assertion (A): DDT is well documented to bioaccumulate in the tissues of many species, including humans – a well-established environmental science fact – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Evaluate Reason (R): DDT’s chemical property of being insoluble in water but soluble in fats/oils is also a well-established fact about its molecular structure – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Check the logical link: does DDT’s fat-solubility/water-insolubility (R) explain why it bioaccumulates (A)? Since fat-soluble, water-insoluble substances are readily stored in fatty tissue and poorly excreted via water-based pathways (urine), this chemical property is precisely what drives bioaccumulation – so (R) directly and correctly explains (A).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> Both (A) and (R) are true, and (R) explains (A) – matches option (1).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The mechanism of bioaccumulation for compounds like DDT is a direct consequence of their fat-solubility: because such compounds dissolve readily in lipids but not in water, organisms cannot efficiently flush them out through normal water-based excretory processes, so they progressively build up in fatty tissue over time. This mechanistic link is exactly what makes (R) the correct explanation of (A), not merely a separately true but unrelated fact.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – Both correct, R explains A</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: DDT’s lipophilic, hydrophobic chemistry (R) directly explains why it bioaccumulates in tissue (A).</li><li><b>Option (2) – Both correct, R does not explain A</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Wrongly denies the clear, well-established causal/mechanistic link between R and A.</li><li><b>Option (3) – A correct, R incorrect</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: R is a well-documented, true chemical property of DDT, not incorrect.</li><li><b>Option (4) – A incorrect, R correct</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: A is a well-documented, true environmental fact about DDT, not incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>DDT bioaccumulates in tissues of many species, including humans.</li><li>DDT is insoluble in water but soluble in fats/oils (lipophilic, hydrophobic).</li><li>This fat-solubility directly explains why DDT accumulates in fatty tissue over time.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'dec23_a',no:40,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement (I):</b> Millennium Development Goals (MDGs) were mainly targeted at developing or poor countries.<br> <b>Statement (II):</b> Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are targeted at all countries whether developed, developing or poor.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the <i>most appropriate</i> answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The <b>Millennium Development Goals (MDGs, 2000–2015)</b> were framed primarily to address poverty, hunger, disease and related challenges predominantly faced by developing and least-developed countries. The <b>Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs, 2015–2030)</b>, which succeeded the MDGs, were deliberately designed as <b>universal</b> goals applicable to <i>all</i> UN member states – developed, developing and poor alike – reflecting a shift toward globally shared responsibility for sustainability.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> Evaluate Statement I: the MDGs’ eight goals (eradicating extreme poverty, achieving universal primary education, etc.) were substantially framed around, and targeted improvements in, developing and poor countries – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> Evaluate Statement II: the SDGs’ 17 goals were explicitly designed as a universal agenda applicable to every country, regardless of development status, unlike the MDGs – <b>True</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> Both statements are independently verified as true – matches option (1).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">A defining, well-documented distinction between the two global frameworks is precisely this shift in scope: MDGs were primarily aimed at developing/poor nations’ challenges, while SDGs deliberately universalised the agenda to include developed nations as well (covering issues like sustainable consumption, climate action and inequality that are relevant to all countries). Both statements accurately capture this well-known distinction.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option (1) – Both correct</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span>: Both statements accurately describe the differing scopes of the MDGs and SDGs.</li><li><b>Option (2) – Both incorrect</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Both statements are, in fact, well-documented and accurate.</li><li><b>Option (3) – I correct, II incorrect</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Statement II is also correct; SDGs are explicitly universal in scope.</li><li><b>Option (4) – I incorrect, II correct</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span>: Statement I is also correct; MDGs were primarily targeted at developing/poor countries.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>MDGs (2000–2015): primarily targeted developing/poor countries.</li><li>SDGs (2015–2030): universal agenda, applicable to all countries – developed, developing and poor.</li><li>This shift in scope (targeted $\\rightarrow$ universal) is a key, frequently-tested distinction between MDGs and SDGs.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 19 Jun 2023 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_e',no:36,q:'When was the Environmental Protection Act (1986) last amended.',opts:['A) 1990','B) 1991','C) 1992','D) 1993'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The <b>Environment (Protection) Act, 1986</b> is India’s umbrella environmental legislation, enacted in the aftermath of the Bhopal Gas Tragedy (1984), giving the Central Government wide powers to protect and improve the environment. It was subsequently amended by the <b>Environment (Protection) Amendment Act, 1991</b>, which, among other changes, enhanced penal provisions.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Given:</b> The Environment (Protection) Act, 1986; required to recall its (last) amendment year.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Recall the legislative timeline:</b> The Act was enacted in 1986 and was amended via the Environment (Protection) Amendment Act in <b>1991</b>.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Eliminate distractors:</b> 1990, 1992 and 1993 do not correspond to a recognised amendment year for this specific Act.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> Option 2 – 1991.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The Environment (Protection) Act, 1986 was amended by the Environment (Protection) Amendment Act, 1991 – this is the standard, textbook amendment year tested in NET’s environmental-legislation questions, matching Option 2.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – 1990 – does not correspond to the recognised amendment year of this Act.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – 1991 – the correct, recognised amendment year.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – 1992 – a plausible-looking but incorrect year; often confused with the Rio Earth Summit (1992), a different environmental milestone.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – 1993 – not a recognised amendment year for this Act.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>"1986 born, 1991 grown"</b> – the Environment (Protection) Act was enacted in 1986 and strengthened (amended) five years later, in 1991.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Environment (Protection) Act enacted 1986, in the aftermath of the Bhopal Gas Tragedy.</li><li>Amended by the Environment (Protection) Amendment Act, <b>1991</b>.</li><li>Do not confuse with the 1992 Rio Earth Summit, a separate international milestone $\\Rightarrow$ Option 2.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_e',no:37,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> Stratospheric ozone is known as ‘good ozone’.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> Ozone at the surface level is a serious health hazard and known as pollutant.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Ozone (O$_3$) plays two very different roles depending on <b>altitude</b>: in the <b>stratosphere</b> (10–50 km up), it forms the "ozone layer" that absorbs harmful UV-B radiation, earning it the label <b>"good ozone."</b> At <b>ground/surface level</b> (troposphere), ozone is a secondary pollutant formed by photochemical reactions between NOx and VOCs in sunlight, and is a serious respiratory health hazard, earning it the label <b>"bad ozone."</b></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Evaluate Statement I:</b> Stratospheric ozone protects life on Earth by absorbing UV-B radiation, and is indeed universally referred to as "good ozone" in environmental science. <b>Statement I is True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Evaluate Statement II:</b> Ground-level ozone is a well-documented respiratory irritant and a recognised air pollutant, often called "bad ozone." <b>Statement II is True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Combine:</b> Both statements are standard, accurate environmental-science facts.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> Option 1 – Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements accurately reflect the well-established "good ozone/bad ozone" distinction based on altitude – stratospheric ozone protects against UV radiation, while ground-level ozone is a health-hazardous pollutant – making Option 1 correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Both correct – accurately reflects the altitude-based good/bad ozone distinction.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Both incorrect – wrongly rejects two well-established environmental-science facts.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – I correct, II incorrect – wrongly rejects Statement II, which is equally well-established.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – I incorrect, II correct – wrongly rejects Statement I, which is equally well-established.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>"High and Helpful, Low and Lethal"</b> – ozone <b>high</b> up (stratosphere) is <b>helpful</b> (blocks UV); ozone <b>low</b> down (ground level) is <b>lethal</b>/harmful (pollutant).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Stratospheric ozone = "good ozone" – absorbs harmful UV-B radiation.</li><li>Ground-level (tropospheric) ozone = "bad ozone" – a health-hazardous secondary pollutant.</li><li>Both statements accurate $\\Rightarrow$ Option 1.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_e',no:38,q:'Many nations blend petrol with ethanol to reduce the emission of CO. The mixture is known as',opts:['A) Gasol','B) Petrohol','C) Gasohol','D) Ethopetrol'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Gasohol</b> is the standard term for a blended fuel of petrol (gasoline) and ethanol – commonly seen as E10, E20 etc. blends (indicating the percentage of ethanol). Blending ethanol into petrol reduces carbon monoxide (CO) emissions because ethanol combustion is more complete (it supplies additional oxygen to the fuel mixture), and it is a key component of India’s Ethanol Blending Programme under its biofuel policy.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Given:</b> A petrol-ethanol blend used to reduce CO emissions; required to name this mixture.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Recall standard terminology:</b> The globally recognised portmanteau term for a gasoline-alcohol (ethanol) blend is "Gasohol" (<b>gas</b>oline $+$ alc<b>ohol</b>).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Eliminate distractors:</b> "Gasol," "Petrohol" and "Ethopetrol" are not standard, recognised terms in fuel-blending literature.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> Option 3 – Gasohol.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">"Gasohol" is the internationally recognised, standard term for a petrol-ethanol fuel blend, directly matching the question’s description and Option 3; the other three options are invented/non-standard terms.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Gasol – not a recognised fuel-blending term.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Petrohol – not a recognised term; a plausible-sounding but invented distractor.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Gasohol – the standard, internationally recognised term for a petrol-ethanol blend.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Ethopetrol – not a recognised term; another plausible-sounding distractor.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>"Gasohol = GAS</b>oline $+$ alc<b>OHOL</b>" – the term is literally built from its two ingredients.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Gasohol = petrol $+$ ethanol blend (e.g., E10, E20).</li><li>Reduces CO emissions due to more complete combustion (ethanol supplies extra oxygen).</li><li>Central to India’s Ethanol Blending Programme $\\Rightarrow$ Option 3.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_e',no:39,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> Screen chamber in wastewater treatment is used to remove small and heavy suspended solids.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> Grit chamber in waste water treatment is used to remove large floating materials.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">In <b>preliminary wastewater treatment</b>, a <b>Screen chamber</b> (bar screens/mesh screens) removes <b>large floating and suspended debris</b> (rags, plastics, wood, large solids) that could damage downstream equipment. A <b>Grit chamber</b>, further along the process, removes <b>small, heavy, inorganic particles</b> (sand, grit, gravel) by allowing them to settle out under reduced flow velocity – the exact <i>opposite</i> pairing to what the two statements in this question describe.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Evaluate Statement I:</b> It claims the Screen chamber removes "small and heavy suspended solids." In reality, this is the function of the <i>Grit chamber</i>, not the Screen chamber (which removes large, often floating, debris). <b>Statement I is False.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Evaluate Statement II:</b> It claims the Grit chamber removes "large floating materials." In reality, this is the function of the <i>Screen chamber</i>, not the Grit chamber. <b>Statement II is False.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Combine:</b> Both statements have exactly swapped the functions of the Screen chamber and the Grit chamber.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> Both statements are incorrect – Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The two statements describe the correct <i>functions</i> of preliminary wastewater treatment but attach each function to the <i>wrong</i> unit – the Screen chamber actually removes large floating debris (not small heavy solids), and the Grit chamber actually removes small heavy solids (not large floating materials) – so both statements as worded are false, making Option 2 correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Both correct – wrongly accepts both swapped-function descriptions as accurate.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Both incorrect – correctly identifies that both statements have swapped the roles of the two treatment units.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – I correct, II incorrect – wrongly treats Statement I as an accurate description of the Screen chamber.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – I incorrect, II correct – correctly flags Statement I as false but wrongly treats Statement II as true.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>"Screen = Sieve the big Stuff; Grit = Grab the tiny, heavy Grains"</b> – Screen chamber catches large floating/suspended debris (like a sieve); Grit chamber settles out small, heavy, sand-like particles.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Screen chamber: removes large floating/suspended debris (rags, plastic, wood).</li><li>Grit chamber: removes small, heavy, inorganic particles (sand, grit) by sedimentation.</li><li>Both statements swap these roles $\\Rightarrow$ both false $\\Rightarrow$ Option 2.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_e',no:40,q:'Which one of the following gases has the maximum Global Warming Potential (GWP)?',opts:['A) Methane (CH$_4$)','B) Nitrous Oxide (N$_2$O)','C) Carbon dioxide (CO$_2$)','D) Carbon Monoxide (CO)'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Global Warming Potential (GWP)</b> measures how much heat a greenhouse gas traps in the atmosphere relative to the same mass of CO$_2$ (which is fixed at GWP $=1$) over a given time horizon (commonly 100 years). Among the commonly-tested greenhouse gases, <b>Nitrous Oxide (N$_2$O)</b> has a very high 100-year GWP (roughly 265–298 times that of CO$_2$), exceeding <b>Methane’s</b> GWP (roughly 28–36 times CO$_2$). <b>Carbon Monoxide (CO)</b> is not typically assigned a standard direct GWP value in the same framework, as its warming effect is mostly indirect (via atmospheric chemistry interactions).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Given:</b> Four gases – CH$_4$, N$_2$O, CO$_2$, CO; required to identify the one with maximum GWP.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Recall reference point:</b> CO$_2$ is the baseline gas, GWP $=1$ by definition – so it cannot be the "maximum."</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Compare CH$_4$ and N$_2$O:</b> Methane’s 100-year GWP is approximately 28–36; Nitrous Oxide’s is substantially higher, approximately 265–298.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Consider CO:</b> Carbon monoxide is not conventionally assigned a comparable direct GWP figure in standard IPCC-style tables; its warming role is indirect.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Compare all four:</b> Among the gases with well-established, high direct GWP values, N$_2$O’s value is clearly the largest.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Conclusion:</b> Option 2 – Nitrous Oxide (N$_2$O).</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Nitrous oxide has a substantially higher 100-year Global Warming Potential than methane, and CO$_2$ is by definition the GWP baseline (=1), while CO does not carry a comparable standard direct GWP figure – so among the four options, N$_2$O has the maximum GWP, matching Option 2.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Methane (CH$_4$) – has a high GWP (28–36) but substantially lower than Nitrous Oxide’s.</li><li><b>Option 2</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>(Correct)</b></span> – Nitrous Oxide (N$_2$O) – has the highest GWP (roughly 265–298) among the four listed gases.</li><li><b>Option 3</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Carbon dioxide (CO$_2$) – is the reference gas, GWP $=1$ by definition; cannot be the maximum.</li><li><b>Option 4</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>(Incorrect)</b></span> – Carbon Monoxide (CO) – not typically assigned a comparable direct GWP value; its warming effect is indirect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Memory trick</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>GWP Ranking (100-yr, approx.):</b> CO$_2$ (1) $&lt;$ Methane ($\\sim$28–36) $&lt;$ <b>Nitrous Oxide ($\\sim$265–298)</b> $\\ll$ many synthetic gases (HFCs, SF$_6$ – far higher still, but not in this option list).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>GWP measures heat-trapping ability relative to CO$_2$ (GWP $=1$).</li><li>Approximate 100-year GWP ranking here: N$_2$O $&gt;$ CH$_4$ $&gt;$ CO$_2$.</li><li>CO does not carry a standard comparable direct GWP value $\\Rightarrow$ Option 2.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 16 Jun 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_a',no:36,q:'When a water body gets enriched with nutrients, the process is called as:',opts:['A) Oligotrophication','B) Eutrophication','C) Mesotrophication','D) Dystrophication'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Eutrophication</b> (Unit-IX: Water pollution) is the process by which a water body becomes progressively enriched with dissolved nutrients (especially nitrogen and phosphorus, often from agricultural runoff or sewage), triggering excessive growth of algae and aquatic plants. The subsequent decomposition of this biomass depletes dissolved oxygen, harming aquatic life. The term derives from the Greek "eu-" (well/thoroughly) + "trophe" (nourishment) – literally, "well-nourished." Oligotrophic water bodies are nutrient-poor (the opposite condition); mesotrophic describes an intermediate nutrient state; dystrophic describes acidic, humic-rich, nutrient-poor waters – none of these describe active nutrient <i>enrichment</i>.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Identify the process described: a water body "gets enriched with nutrients."</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Recall that the term for this specific nutrient-enrichment process, and its ecological consequences (algal blooms, oxygen depletion), is eutrophication.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Eliminate the distractors: oligotrophic describes low-nutrient conditions (the opposite); mesotrophic describes a moderate/intermediate nutrient state, not the process of becoming enriched; dystrophic describes acidic, nutrient-poor conditions.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Select Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Option 2 (Eutrophication) is correct because it is the precise, standard ecological/environmental-science term for the process of nutrient enrichment of a water body and its resulting excessive biological productivity, exactly as described in the question.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Oligotrophication):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Describes a nutrient-<i>poor</i> condition/trend, the opposite of enrichment.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Eutrophication):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – The correct term for nutrient enrichment of a water body.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Mesotrophication):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Refers to an intermediate nutrient state, not the active process of becoming enriched.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Dystrophication):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Refers to acidic, humic, nutrient-poor water conditions, unrelated to nutrient enrichment.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Eutrophication = nutrient enrichment of water $\\to$ algal blooms $\\to$ oxygen depletion $\\to$ harm to aquatic life.</li><li>Oligotrophic = nutrient-poor; Mesotrophic = intermediate; Dystrophic = acidic/humic, nutrient-poor.</li><li>Correct answer: <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_a',no:37,q:'Which of the following statements are correct in respect of Green House Gases?<br><br>A. They are generally of light green colour<br> B. They absorb thermal radiations emitted by earth.<br> C. Water vapour is a green house gas.<br> D. Green house gases cause global warming.<br> E. Ammonia (NH$_3$) is a green house gas.<br><br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C, D Only','B) A, C, E Only','C) B, C, D Only','D) A, B, D, E Only'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Greenhouse gases (GHGs) (Unit-IX: Climate change) are atmospheric gases – <b>colourless and invisible</b> to the naked eye, not "light green" – that trap heat by <b>absorbing and re-emitting infrared/thermal radiation</b> emitted from the Earth’s surface, producing the greenhouse effect and driving global warming when their concentrations rise. Recognised major GHGs include carbon dioxide, methane, nitrous oxide, and critically, <b>water vapour</b> (the most abundant natural GHG). Ammonia is a reactive atmospheric gas involved in aerosol formation and nitrogen deposition but is <i>not</i> conventionally classified among the significant greenhouse gases in standard environmental science.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Evaluate each statement against established GHG science.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> A (light green colour) – false; GHGs are colourless/invisible gases, "green" in "greenhouse" refers to the greenhouse-glass analogy, not gas colour. B (absorb thermal radiation from earth) – true; this is the defining mechanism of the greenhouse effect. C (water vapour is a GHG) – true; it is in fact the most abundant greenhouse gas. D (GHGs cause global warming) – true; this is the well-established consequence of rising GHG concentrations. E (ammonia is a GHG) – false; ammonia is not conventionally classified as a significant greenhouse gas.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Collect the true statements: B, C, D.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Match to Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Option 3 (B, C, D Only) is correct because these three statements accurately describe the defining mechanism (absorbing thermal radiation), a key example (water vapour), and the major consequence (global warming) of greenhouse gases, while Statement A misattributes a physical colour to GHGs and Statement E misclassifies ammonia as a greenhouse gas.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A, B, C, D Only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly includes A (light green colour), a factually false description of GHGs, alongside three correct statements.</li><li><b>Option 2 (A, C, E Only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly includes both false statements (A and E) while omitting two genuinely correct ones (B and D).</li><li><b>Option 3 (B, C, D Only):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – Correctly selects exactly the three scientifically accurate statements.</li><li><b>Option 4 (A, B, D, E Only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly includes both false statements (A and E) alongside two correct ones (B and D), while omitting the correct statement C.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>GHGs are colourless; they absorb and re-emit thermal (infrared) radiation from Earth, driving global warming.</li><li>Water vapour is the most abundant natural greenhouse gas.</li><li>Ammonia is not conventionally classified as a major greenhouse gas.</li><li>Correct set: B, C, D $\\Rightarrow$ <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_a',no:38,q:'Which of the following air pollutants help in the formation of photochemical smog?',opts:['A) Carbon monoxide','B) methane','C) Oxides of Sulphur','D) Oxides of nitrogen'],ans:0,alt:[3],exp:'<div class="p1-note">ℹ NTA accepted more than one option as correct for this question (Option A and Option D).</div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Photochemical smog</b> (Unit-IX: Air pollution) forms when <b>oxides of nitrogen (NO$_x$)</b> react with volatile organic compounds (VOCs) in the presence of sunlight (UV radiation), producing ground-level ozone, peroxyacetyl nitrate (PAN), and other secondary pollutants – NO$_x$ is the textbook primary driver of this reaction chain and is the scientifically unambiguous answer. <b>Carbon monoxide</b>, while not a primary photochemical precursor in the core NO$_x$–VOC–sunlight reaction cycle, does participate in the atmosphere’s photochemistry by reacting with hydroxyl radicals (OH), thereby influencing the oxidising capacity of the air mass and indirectly affecting ozone/smog formation – some standard textbooks list CO alongside NO$_x$ and hydrocarbons as contributing pollutants in photochemical smog formation. <b>Methane</b> and <b>oxides of sulphur</b> are not associated with photochemical smog: methane is a greenhouse gas with a much slower reaction pathway, and oxides of sulphur are chiefly linked to acid rain and <i>industrial/sulphurous</i> smog, a chemically distinct phenomenon from photochemical smog.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the defining chemistry of photochemical smog: NO$_x$ + VOCs + sunlight (UV) $\\to$ ozone + PAN + other secondary oxidants.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Evaluate each pollutant: Oxides of nitrogen – the core, textbook-primary precursor, definitely correct; Carbon monoxide – a contributing pollutant in the broader photochemical reaction system (via OH-radical chemistry), included in several standard references on photochemical smog; Methane – primarily a greenhouse gas, not a recognised photochemical-smog precursor; Oxides of Sulphur – primarily associated with acid rain and industrial (sulphurous) smog, a different phenomenon.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Since both Carbon monoxide and Oxides of nitrogen are defensible, textbook-supportable answers (one as the core driver, one as a recognised contributing pollutant), the officially revised key accepted <i>both</i> Option 1 and Option 4 as correct, awarding marks to candidates who selected either.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Final accepted answer: <b>Options 1 and 4</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both options are accepted because Oxides of nitrogen are the unambiguous, core scientific driver of photochemical smog formation (via the NO$_x$–VOC–sunlight reaction cycle), while Carbon monoxide is recognised in several standard environmental-science references as a contributing pollutant to the same photochemical reaction system through its role in atmospheric radical chemistry – the ambiguity between "core driver" and "contributing pollutant" led the examining body to award marks for both options.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Carbon monoxide):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> (accepted) – A recognised contributing pollutant in photochemical reaction chemistry via OH-radical interactions.</li><li><b>Option 2 (methane):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – A significant greenhouse gas, but not a standard precursor of photochemical smog.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Oxides of Sulphur):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Associated with acid rain and industrial/sulphurous smog, a chemically distinct phenomenon from photochemical smog.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Oxides of nitrogen):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – The core, primary precursor pollutant in the defining NO$_x$–VOC–sunlight photochemical reaction.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Photochemical smog core reaction: NO$_x$ + VOCs + sunlight $\\to$ ozone + PAN.</li><li>Oxides of nitrogen = the unambiguous primary precursor (Option 4).</li><li>Carbon monoxide = a recognised contributing pollutant via atmospheric radical chemistry (Option 1) – both accepted in the revised key.</li><li>Oxides of sulphur $\\to$ acid rain/industrial smog; Methane $\\to$ greenhouse gas – neither is linked to photochemical smog.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_a',no:39,q:'Through enrichment process, concentration of which uranium isotope is increased in the natural uranium?',opts:['A) U-234','B) U-235','C) U-238','D) U-239'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Natural uranium is composed overwhelmingly of the isotope <b>U-238</b> ($\\approx99.3\\%$) with only a small fraction of the <b>fissile</b> isotope <b>U-235</b> ($\\approx0.7\\%$), plus a trace of U-234. <b>Uranium enrichment</b> (Unit-IX: Nuclear energy resources) is the industrial process (e.g., via gas centrifuges) that increases the proportion of <b>U-235</b> relative to U-238, because U-235 is the isotope capable of sustaining a nuclear fission chain reaction, making it essential for nuclear reactor fuel and weapons-grade material. U-239 is not a naturally significant uranium isotope; it is a short-lived intermediate product formed when U-238 absorbs a neutron (on its way to becoming plutonium-239).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the natural isotopic composition of uranium: predominantly U-238, with a small fraction of fissile U-235.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Recall the purpose of the enrichment process: to increase the concentration of the fissile isotope needed to sustain a nuclear chain reaction.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Identify U-235 as the fissile isotope whose proportion enrichment specifically increases, relative to the non-fissile U-238.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Select Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Option 2 (U-235) is correct because uranium enrichment is, by definition, the process of increasing the relative concentration of the fissile isotope U-235 (naturally only about 0.7% of uranium) so that the resulting material can sustain the nuclear fission chain reaction required for reactor fuel or weapons use.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (U-234):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – A trace natural isotope, not the target of the enrichment process.</li><li><b>Option 2 (U-235):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – The fissile isotope whose concentration enrichment is specifically designed to increase.</li><li><b>Option 3 (U-238):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – The dominant, non-fissile natural isotope; enrichment <i>reduces</i> its relative proportion, not increases it.</li><li><b>Option 4 (U-239):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Not a naturally significant uranium isotope; it is a short-lived product of neutron capture by U-238, unrelated to the enrichment process.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Natural uranium: $\\approx$99.3% U-238, $\\approx$0.7% U-235 (fissile).</li><li>Enrichment increases the proportion of fissile U-235 needed for chain reactions.</li><li>Correct answer: <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_a',no:40,q:'Three ‘Rs’ used for the mitigation of solid wastes are<br><br>A. Recover<br> B. Reduce<br> C. Reuse<br> D. Restrict<br> E. Recycle<br><br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C Only','B) B, C, D Only','C) B, C, E Only','D) C, D, E Only'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The standard, globally recognised waste-management hierarchy "3 R’s" (Unit-IX: Waste mitigation) are: <b>Reduce</b> (minimise waste generation at source), <b>Reuse</b> (use items again for the same or a different purpose without reprocessing), and <b>Recycle</b> (reprocess waste materials into new products). "Recover" (energy/material recovery) and "Restrict" are related waste-management concepts but are not part of the canonical, universally-cited "3 R’s" framework.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the standard, textbook "3 R’s" of solid waste management: Reduce, Reuse, Recycle.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Match these three terms to the lettered list: Reduce $=$ B, Reuse $=$ C, Recycle $=$ E.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Confirm that Recover (A) and Restrict (D) are not part of the canonical 3 R’s framework, though they are related waste-management ideas.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Select "B, C, E Only" – Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Option 3 (B, C, E Only) is correct because Reduce, Reuse, and Recycle are precisely and universally recognised as the "3 R’s" of solid waste mitigation, forming the standard waste-management hierarchy taught in environmental science, whereas Recover and Restrict, while related concepts, are not part of this specific canonical triad.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A, B, C Only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly includes Recover (A) in place of Recycle (E); tests precise recall of the canonical three terms.</li><li><b>Option 2 (B, C, D Only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly includes Restrict (D) in place of Recycle (E).</li><li><b>Option 3 (B, C, E Only):</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>✓ Correct</b></span> – Correctly identifies Reduce, Reuse, and Recycle as the standard 3 R’s.</li><li><b>Option 4 (C, D, E Only):</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>× Incorrect</b></span> – Incorrectly includes Restrict (D) in place of Reduce (B), while correctly including Reuse and Recycle.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>The canonical "3 R’s" of solid waste management: Reduce, Reuse, Recycle.</li><li>"Recover" and "Restrict" are related but not part of this specific canonical triad.</li><li>Correct answer: <b>Option 3</b> (B, C, E Only).</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 15 Jun 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_b',no:36,q:'Given below are two statements:<br> <b>Statement I:</b> Chlorine is one of the most commonly used and inexpensive chemical disinfectants for water.<br> <b>Statement II:</b> Optimum Fluoride levels in drinking water help prevent cavities in children.<br> In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are correct','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect','C) Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect','D) Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Chlorination</b> is the most widespread and cost-effective method of disinfecting drinking water supplies against pathogens. <b>Fluoride</b>, at an optimum concentration in drinking water, is well established (WHO, public-health dentistry) to strengthen tooth enamel and significantly reduce the incidence of dental caries (cavities), particularly in children – both well-established public-health facts under Unit-IX (Impacts of pollutants/water quality on human health).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Evaluate Statement I – chlorine is indeed the most common, low-cost chemical disinfectant used worldwide for water treatment. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Evaluate Statement II – optimum (not excessive) fluoride levels in drinking water are well documented to help prevent dental cavities in children. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 (Correct option):</b> Both statements correct $\\Rightarrow$ Option 1.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both statements describe well-established, textbook public-health facts: chlorine’s role as the cheapest, most widely used water disinfectant, and optimum fluoride’s protective role against cavities – both are independently verifiable and correct, supporting Option 1.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Both correct) – Correct:</b> Chlorination as the standard cheap disinfectant, and optimum fluoride’s cavity-prevention role, are both well-established facts.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Both incorrect):</b> Contradicted by well-established public-health knowledge on both counts; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3 (I correct, II incorrect):</b> Wrongly rejects Statement II, which is also well-established; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4 (I incorrect, II correct):</b> Wrongly rejects Statement I, which is also well-established; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Chlorine: most common, inexpensive chemical disinfectant for water treatment.</li><li>Optimum (not excessive) fluoride in drinking water helps prevent dental cavities in children.</li><li>Correct answer: Both statements correct $\\rightarrow$ Option 1.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_b',no:37,q:'What is the full form of NBOD?',opts:['A) Neutral Biochemical Oxygen Demand','B) Natural Biochemical Oxygen Demand','C) Normalised Biochemical Oxygen Demand','D) Nitrogenous Biochemical Oxygen Demand'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">Total Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) of water/wastewater has two components: <b>CBOD</b> (Carbonaceous BOD, from oxidation of organic carbon compounds) and <b>NBOD</b> (<b>Nitrogenous</b> BOD, from the biological oxidation/nitrification of ammonia and organic nitrogen into nitrite and nitrate). NBOD is a standard water-quality/pollution-monitoring term under Unit-IX.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall that BOD testing distinguishes oxygen consumed by carbon-based microbial respiration (CBOD) from oxygen consumed by nitrification of nitrogen compounds (NBOD).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> “N” in NBOD specifically denotes <b>Nitrogenous</b>, referring to the nitrogen-compound-driven oxygen demand.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 (Correct option):</b> Nitrogenous Biochemical Oxygen Demand matches Option 4.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">NBOD is a well-defined, standard water-quality parameter representing specifically the oxygen consumed during microbial nitrification (ammonia $\\to$ nitrite $\\to$ nitrate), which is why “Nitrogenous” – not “Neutral”, “Natural”, or “Normalised” – is the correct expansion.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Neutral BOD):</b> Not a standard or recognised water-quality term; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Natural BOD):</b> Not a standard or recognised water-quality term; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Normalised BOD):</b> Not a standard or recognised water-quality term; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Nitrogenous BOD) – Correct:</b> The standard, internationally recognised expansion of NBOD, referring to oxygen demand from nitrification.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Total BOD $=$ CBOD (carbonaceous) $+$ NBOD (nitrogenous).</li><li>NBOD arises from microbial nitrification of ammonia/organic nitrogen to nitrite/nitrate.</li><li>Correct answer: Nitrogenous Biochemical Oxygen Demand $\\rightarrow$ Option 4.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_b',no:38,q:'Soot Particles are <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> are also called black carbon</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> are mostly of coarse size range</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> are toxic</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> contribute in global warming</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> can penetrate in to deeper parts of lungs</li></ol> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C, D only','B) A, B, C, E only','C) A, C, D, E only','D) B, C, D, E only'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Soot</b> particles are a form of <b>black carbon</b>, a significant <b>fine</b> particulate-matter (predominantly PM$_{2.5}$-range, i.e. <i>fine</i>, not coarse) air pollutant. They are toxic to human health, act as a potent short-lived climate forcer contributing to <b>global warming</b>, and – because of their small (fine) size – are able to <b>penetrate deep into the lungs</b> (and even the bloodstream) upon inhalation.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Evaluate Statement A – soot is scientifically synonymous with black carbon. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Evaluate Statement B – soot/black carbon particles are predominantly in the <i>fine</i> particulate size range (PM$_{2.5}$ and smaller), not coarse. <b>False.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Evaluate Statement C – soot particles are established respiratory and cardiovascular toxicants. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Evaluate Statement D – black carbon is recognised as a major short-lived climate pollutant, contributing significantly to atmospheric warming. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Evaluate Statement E – their small (fine) size allows soot particles to penetrate deep into the lungs’ alveolar regions. <b>True.</b></li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6 (Correct option):</b> A, C, D, E only matches Option 3.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The only false statement is B: soot/black carbon is a <i>fine</i>, not coarse, particulate pollutant – this fine size is in fact precisely why it is toxic (C), a potent climate forcer (D), and able to penetrate deep into the lungs (E); excluding B alone leaves A, C, D, E as the correct set.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A, B, C, D only):</b> Wrongly includes B (soot is fine, not coarse) and omits E; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2 (A, B, C, E only):</b> Wrongly includes B and omits D; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3 (A, C, D, E only) – Correct:</b> Correctly excludes only the false statement B (coarse size), retaining all four true statements.</li><li><b>Option 4 (B, C, D, E only):</b> Wrongly includes B and omits A, the basic identification of soot as black carbon; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Soot $=$ black carbon $=$ a <i>fine</i> (not coarse) particulate pollutant.</li><li>Toxic, contributes to global warming (short-lived climate forcer), penetrates deep into lungs due to small size.</li><li>Correct answer: A, C, D, E only $\\rightarrow$ Option 3.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_b',no:39,q:'Ozone layer which protects us from harmful ultraviolet radiations is situated in',opts:['A) Troposphere','B) Stratosphere','C) Mesosphere','D) Ionosphere'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The atmosphere’s protective <b>ozone layer</b>, which absorbs the majority of the Sun’s harmful ultraviolet (UV-B and UV-C) radiation, is located in the <b>stratosphere</b>, roughly 15–35 km above the Earth’s surface – above the troposphere (where weather occurs) and below the mesosphere and ionosphere.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the vertical layering of Earth’s atmosphere: Troposphere (lowest, weather layer) $\\to$ Stratosphere (contains the ozone layer) $\\to$ Mesosphere $\\to$ Thermosphere/Ionosphere.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> The ozone concentration peak, forming the protective “ozone layer”, is located within the stratosphere.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 (Correct option):</b> Stratosphere matches Option 2.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The stratosphere’s relatively stable, stratified structure allows ozone (O$_3$) to accumulate and persist at a concentration high enough to absorb most incoming UV radiation, which is why the ozone layer is specifically associated with this atmospheric layer rather than the troposphere, mesosphere, or ionosphere.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Troposphere):</b> The lowest atmospheric layer, where weather occurs; ozone concentration here is low and this is not the protective ozone layer; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Stratosphere) – Correct:</b> Location of the concentrated, protective ozone layer that absorbs harmful UV radiation.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Mesosphere):</b> A higher atmospheric layer, not the location of the main protective ozone concentration; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Ionosphere):</b> An electrically charged upper-atmosphere region (overlapping the thermosphere), not the ozone layer’s location; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Atmospheric layering (low to high): Troposphere, Stratosphere, Mesosphere, Thermosphere/Ionosphere.</li><li>Protective ozone layer $=$ located in the Stratosphere.</li><li>Correct answer: Stratosphere $\\rightarrow$ Option 2.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_b',no:40,q:'The latest amendment to Montreal protocol is -',opts:['A) Kigali Amendment','B) Nairobi Amendment','C) Copenhagen Amendment','D) Bangkok Amendment'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b">The <b>Montreal Protocol</b> (1987) regulates the phase-out of ozone-depleting substances. Its most recent amendment, the <b>Kigali Amendment</b> (adopted 2016, in force from 2019), extends the Protocol’s scope to phase <i>down</i> <b>hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs)</b> – potent greenhouse gases used as replacements for earlier ozone-depleting refrigerants – linking ozone protection policy directly to climate change mitigation.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the chronology of Montreal Protocol amendments: London (1990), Copenhagen (1992), Montreal (1997), Beijing (1999), and most recently Kigali (2016).</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> The Kigali Amendment is the latest in this sequence, specifically targeting HFCs for phase-down.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3 (Correct option):</b> Kigali Amendment matches Option 1.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Among the listed options, only Kigali (2016) is an actual, and the most recent, amendment to the Montreal Protocol; Nairobi and Bangkok are cities associated with other environmental meetings/conventions (not Montreal Protocol amendments), and Copenhagen, while a genuine 1992 amendment, predates Kigali by over two decades.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Kigali Amendment) – Correct:</b> The most recent (2016) amendment to the Montreal Protocol, targeting HFC phase-down.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Nairobi Amendment):</b> Not an actual Montreal Protocol amendment; Nairobi is associated with other environmental governance bodies (e.g. UNEP headquarters); incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Copenhagen Amendment):</b> A genuine but much earlier (1992) Montreal Protocol amendment, not the latest; incorrect.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Bangkok Amendment):</b> Not an actual Montreal Protocol amendment; incorrect.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Montreal Protocol amendments (chronological): London 1990, Copenhagen 1992, Montreal 1997, Beijing 1999, Kigali 2016.</li><li>Kigali Amendment (2016) $=$ latest; targets phase-down of HFCs, linking ozone protection with climate action.</li><li>Correct answer: Kigali Amendment $\\rightarrow$ Option 1.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 13 Jun 2023 · Evening Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_c',no:36,q:'Air Quality Index (AQI) is represented in',opts:['A) Unit of $\\mu$g/m<sup>3</sup> (microgram per meter cube)','B) Unit of ppm','C) Percentage (%)','D) Numbers'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Air Quality Index (AQI).</b> While the underlying concentrations of individual pollutants (such as PM2.5, PM10, SO$_2$, NO$_2$, CO, O$_3$) are measured in physical units like $\\mu$g/m$^3$ or ppm, the <b>AQI itself</b> is a standardised, dimensionless composite index that converts these raw pollutant concentrations into a single <b>number</b> (typically on a 0–500 scale) for easy public communication of overall air quality (e.g., Good, Moderate, Poor, Severe categories are assigned to numeric ranges).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Distinguish AQI from raw pollutant measurement:</b> Individual pollutant concentrations are indeed measured in units like $\\mu$g/m$^3$ or ppm, but the AQI is a derived, standardised composite value calculated <i>from</i> these raw measurements. <b>Recall how AQI is reported:</b> AQI is reported as a single dimensionless number (e.g., an AQI of 150), not with a concentration unit attached, allowing it to summarise multiple pollutants on one common numeric scale. <b>Eliminate the unit-based options:</b> Options citing $\\mu$g/m$^3$, ppm, or percentage describe raw pollutant concentration units, not how the composite AQI value itself is expressed. <b>Conclusion:</b> AQI is represented in Numbers – Option 4.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Although AQI is calculated using pollutant concentrations expressed in units such as $\\mu$g/m$^3$ or ppm, the index itself is reported as a plain <b>number</b> on a standard scale, making it easy to communicate air quality to the public without requiring knowledge of individual pollutant units. Option 4 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 ($\\mu$g/m$^3$) – Incorrect.</b> This is the unit used for raw pollutant <i>concentration</i> (e.g., PM2.5 levels), not the unit in which the composite AQI value itself is reported.</li><li><b>Option 2 (ppm) – Incorrect.</b> This is another raw-concentration unit (used, e.g., for gaseous pollutants), not how the final AQI value is expressed.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Percentage) – Incorrect.</b> AQI is not expressed as a percentage of any total; it is a standalone numeric index.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Numbers) – Correct.</b> AQI is reported as a single number (commonly on a 0–500 scale), summarising multiple pollutant readings into one composite value.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Individual pollutants (PM2.5, SO$_2$, etc.) are measured in $\\mu$g/m$^3$ or ppm.</li><li>AQI itself is a derived, dimensionless <b>number</b> (commonly 0–500) summarising these measurements.</li><li>AQI numeric ranges map to descriptive categories (Good, Satisfactory, Moderate, Poor, Very Poor, Severe).</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_c',no:37,q:'Given below are two statements: Statement I: Concentration of Formaldehyde in indoor environment can be thousand times higher than outdoor environment. Statement II: Formaldehyde is a known carcinogen. In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.',opts:['A) Both Statement I and Statement II are true','B) Both Statement I and Statement II are false','C) Statement I is true but Statement II is false','D) Statement I is false but Statement II is true'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Indoor Air Pollution – Formaldehyde.</b> Formaldehyde is a common indoor air pollutant, off-gassed from furniture, pressed-wood products, adhesives, paints and building materials; its concentration indoors is well documented to be dramatically higher than outdoors – often cited as being up to a thousand times higher in poorly ventilated indoor spaces. Formaldehyde is also officially classified as a <b>known human carcinogen</b> by international bodies such as the International Agency for Research on Cancer (IARC).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Evaluate Statement I:</b> It is a well-documented environmental-science fact that formaldehyde levels indoors, due to off-gassing from furniture and building materials, can be up to a thousand times higher than outdoor levels; Statement I is <b>True</b>. <b>Evaluate Statement II:</b> Formaldehyde is internationally recognised (e.g., by IARC) as a known human carcinogen; Statement II is <b>True</b>. <b>Combine the evaluations:</b> Both statements are independently accurate, well-established facts about formaldehyde as an indoor air pollutant. <b>Conclusion:</b> Both Statement I and Statement II are true – Option 1.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Both the dramatically elevated indoor concentration of formaldehyde relative to outdoor air, and its classification as a known carcinogen, are well-established environmental-health facts. Option 1 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Both true) – Correct.</b> Both statements are accurate, well-documented facts about formaldehyde as an indoor pollutant.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Both false) – Incorrect.</b> Both statements are, in fact, well-established true facts.</li><li><b>Option 3 (I true, II false) – Incorrect.</b> Statement II is also true – formaldehyde is indeed a recognised carcinogen.</li><li><b>Option 4 (I false, II true) – Incorrect.</b> Statement I is also true – indoor formaldehyde levels are indeed documented to be far higher than outdoor levels.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Formaldehyde indoor concentration can be up to 1000 times higher than outdoors (off-gassing from furniture, adhesives, pressed wood).</li><li>Formaldehyde is classified as a known human carcinogen (IARC classification).</li><li>Indoor air pollution is frequently a more severe health hazard than outdoor pollution due to poor ventilation and material off-gassing.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_c',no:38,q:'In water treatment, Aeration is the process which',opts:['A) Removes excess and objectionable gases','B) Readjusts the pH of water','C) Removes the large particles of floc from water','D) Removes the solid particles'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Water Treatment – Aeration.</b> Aeration is a water-treatment process in which air (oxygen) is deliberately introduced into water, primarily to <b>remove dissolved gases</b> that cause bad odour/taste (such as hydrogen sulphide and excess carbon dioxide) and to help oxidise dissolved substances like iron and manganese so that they can subsequently be removed. It is distinct from pH-adjustment (typically via chemical dosing), floc removal (via sedimentation/settling), and solids removal (via filtration/sedimentation).</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Recall the standard water-treatment process sequence:</b> Aeration $\\rightarrow$ Coagulation/Flocculation $\\rightarrow$ Sedimentation (removes floc) $\\rightarrow$ Filtration (removes remaining solids) $\\rightarrow$ Disinfection; pH adjustment is generally a separate chemical-dosing step. <b>Identify aeration’s specific role in this sequence:</b> Aeration’s defined purpose is to introduce air/oxygen into the water to strip out dissolved objectionable gases and odour-causing compounds, and to aid oxidation of iron/manganese. <b>Eliminate the other options as belonging to different stages:</b> pH readjustment is a separate chemical step; removing large floc particles is the role of sedimentation (after coagulation/flocculation); removing solid particles generally is the role of filtration – none of these is the defining purpose of aeration. <b>Conclusion:</b> Aeration removes excess and objectionable gases – Option 1.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Aeration’s defined role in the water-treatment process is to introduce air into water to strip out dissolved, objectionable gases (and assist in oxidising certain dissolved metals) – not to adjust pH, remove floc, or remove general solids, which are the roles of other, separate treatment steps. Option 1 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Removes excess and objectionable gases) – Correct.</b> This is aeration’s defined purpose – introducing air to strip out dissolved gases and odour-causing compounds.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Readjusts pH) – Incorrect.</b> pH adjustment is typically achieved through separate chemical dosing, not through aeration.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Removes large floc particles) – Incorrect.</b> Floc removal is the role of the sedimentation stage, which follows coagulation/flocculation, not aeration.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Removes solid particles) – Incorrect.</b> General solids removal is primarily achieved through filtration and sedimentation, not aeration.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Aeration = introducing air/oxygen into water to remove dissolved, objectionable gases (odour/taste) and assist oxidation of iron/manganese.</li><li>Standard sequence: Aeration $\\rightarrow$ Coagulation/Flocculation $\\rightarrow$ Sedimentation $\\rightarrow$ Filtration $\\rightarrow$ Disinfection.</li><li>Do not confuse aeration’s gas-removal role with the separate roles of sedimentation (floc removal) or filtration (solids removal).</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_c',no:39,q:'Which of the following are classified as hazardous substances? A. Activated substances B. Ignitable substances C. Corrosive substances D. Reactive substances E. Toxic substances. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C, D only','B) B, C, D, E only','C) A, C, D, E only','D) A, B, D, E only'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Standard Hazardous-Waste Characteristics.</b> Under the widely used regulatory classification (such as the US EPA’s RCRA framework, echoed in general environmental-science syllabi), a substance/waste is classified as hazardous if it exhibits one or more of exactly four characteristics: <b>Ignitability</b> (easily catches fire), <b>Corrosivity</b> (extreme acidity/alkalinity that corrodes materials or tissue), <b>Reactivity</b> (unstable, may explode or react violently/release toxic gases), and <b>Toxicity</b> (harmful or fatal if ingested/absorbed). ‘Activated’ is not one of these recognised hazard characteristics.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Recall the standard four hazard characteristics:</b> Ignitability, Corrosivity, Reactivity, Toxicity – often remembered together as the defining criteria for classifying hazardous waste. <b>Match the given letters to these characteristics:</b> B = Ignitable, C = Corrosive, D = Reactive, E = Toxic – all four match the standard classification exactly. <b>Evaluate ’Activated substances’ (A):</b> ‘Activated’ is not a recognised hazard characteristic in this standard framework, so A must be excluded. <b>Conclusion:</b> B, C, D, E only – Option 2.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The four standard, internationally recognised characteristics used to classify a substance as hazardous are ignitability, corrosivity, reactivity and toxicity – exactly options B, C, D and E. ‘Activated substances’ is not one of these recognised categories, so Option 2 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A, B, C, D only) – Incorrect.</b> Wrongly includes A (‘Activated,’ not a recognised hazard category) and omits E (Toxic, which is a recognised category).</li><li><b>Option 2 (B, C, D, E only) – Correct.</b> Exactly matches the standard four hazard characteristics: Ignitable, Corrosive, Reactive, Toxic.</li><li><b>Option 3 (A, C, D, E only) – Incorrect.</b> Wrongly includes A and omits B (Ignitable), which is a recognised category.</li><li><b>Option 4 (A, B, D, E only) – Incorrect.</b> Wrongly includes A and omits C (Corrosive), which is a recognised category.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Standard hazardous-substance characteristics: Ignitable, Corrosive, Reactive, Toxic (‘ICRT’).</li><li>‘Activated substances’ is not a recognised hazard-classification category.</li><li>Mnemonic: think ‘I Can React Toxically’ for Ignitable, Corrosive, Reactive, Toxic.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_c',no:40,q:'What is the limit of water availability for a region to be classified as water-stressed region?',opts:['A) Less than 1700 m<sup>3</sup>/ person/year','B) Less than 2000 m<sup>3</sup>/ person/year','C) Less than 2300 m<sup>3</sup>/ person/year','D) Less than 2500 m<sup>3</sup>/ person/year'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Falkenmark Water Stress Indicator.</b> The internationally accepted Falkenmark indicator classifies a region as <b>water-stressed</b> when annual renewable water availability falls below <b>1,700 m$^3$ per person per year</b>. (Further, availability below 1,000 m$^3$/person/year is classified as water <i>scarcity</i>, and below 500 m$^3$/person/year as <i>absolute</i> water scarcity.)</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-step solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><b>Recall the standard international threshold:</b> The Falkenmark Water Stress Index defines water stress at annual per-capita availability below 1,700 m$^3$. <b>Compare with the given options:</b> Among the choices (1700, 2000, 2300, 2500 m$^3$/person/year), only 1,700 m$^3$ matches this internationally accepted threshold. <b>Note the related, stricter thresholds for context:</b> Below 1,000 m$^3$/person/year = water scarcity; below 500 m$^3$/person/year = absolute scarcity – both stricter than the 1,700 m$^3$ water-stress threshold. <b>Conclusion:</b> Less than 1700 m$^3$/person/year is the correct threshold – Option 1.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The universally cited Falkenmark indicator defines the water-stress threshold at 1,700 m$^3$ of renewable water availability per person per year; regions falling below this level are classified as water-stressed. Option 1 is correct.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Less than 1700 m$^3$) – Correct.</b> This matches the internationally accepted Falkenmark water-stress threshold exactly.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Less than 2000 m$^3$) – Incorrect.</b> Does not match the standard threshold; a common distractor figure close to, but not exactly matching, 1,700.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Less than 2300 m$^3$) – Incorrect.</b> Not the standard, internationally recognised threshold value.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Less than 2500 m$^3$) – Incorrect.</b> Also not the correct, internationally recognised figure.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Falkenmark Water Stress Indicator: water stress $=$ below 1,700 m$^3$/person/year.</li><li>Water scarcity = below 1,000 m$^3$/person/year; absolute scarcity = below 500 m$^3$/person/year.</li><li>Memorise the exact figure (1,700) precisely – these threshold questions are easy marks if the number is recalled correctly.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  // ── 13 Jun 2023 · Morning Shift ──
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_d',no:36,q:'Secretariate of convention on Biodiversity (CBD) is situated in',opts:['A) Geneva, Switzerland','B) New York, United States','C) London, England','D) Montreal, Canada'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">This is a direct <b>factual recall</b> question about international environmental agreements, part of Unit 9’s coverage of “International agreements/efforts – Montreal Protocol, Rio Summit, Convention on Biodiversity, Kyoto Protocol, Paris Agreement, International Solar Alliance.” The Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD), adopted at the 1992 Rio Earth Summit, has its Secretariat headquartered in Montreal, Canada.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the host city for the CBD Secretariat, established following the 1992 Rio Earth Summit.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> The CBD Secretariat operates under UNEP (United Nations Environment Programme) administrative arrangements, and its headquarters were established in Montreal, Canada.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Eliminate Geneva, Switzerland – home to numerous UN bodies (e.g., WHO, WTO, UNHCR) but not the CBD Secretariat.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Eliminate New York, United States – home to UN Headquarters (General Assembly, Security Council) but not the CBD Secretariat.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Eliminate London, England – not associated with hosting the CBD Secretariat.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Confirm Montreal, Canada, as the correct, factually accurate host city.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> Correct option: <b>Option 4</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">The Secretariat of the Convention on Biological Diversity has been headquartered in Montreal, Canada, since the Convention’s entry into force, making Option 4 the factually correct answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Geneva, Switzerland)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Geneva hosts many international bodies but not the CBD Secretariat.</li><li><b>Option 2 (New York, United States)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Home to the UN’s main headquarters, but not the specific CBD Secretariat.</li><li><b>Option 3 (London, England)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Not associated with hosting the CBD Secretariat.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Montreal, Canada)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – The factually correct host city of the CBD Secretariat.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>CBD (Convention on Biological Diversity) Secretariat is located in <b>Montreal, Canada</b>.</li><li>CBD was adopted at the 1992 Rio Earth Summit.</li><li>Correct answer: <b>Option 4</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_d',no:37,q:'Which one of the following is included while estimating Air Quality Index (AQI)?',opts:['A) N<sub>2</sub>O Nitrous oxide','B) NO<sub>2</sub> Nitrogen dioxide','C) N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> Dinitrogen trioxide','D) N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> Dinitrogen pentoxide'],ans:1,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">India’s <b>Air Quality Index (AQI)</b>, developed by the Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB), is calculated from a defined set of standard pollutants: PM<sub>2.5</sub>, PM<sub>10</sub>, NO<sub>2</sub> (nitrogen dioxide), SO<sub>2</sub> (sulphur dioxide), CO (carbon monoxide), O<sub>3</sub> (ozone), NH<sub>3</sub> (ammonia), and Pb (lead). Other nitrogen-oxide compounds such as N<sub>2</sub>O, N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, and N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> are not part of this standard AQI pollutant set.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the CPCB’s official list of pollutants used to compute the AQI.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Evaluate N<sub>2</sub>O (nitrous oxide) – a greenhouse gas but not among the standard CPCB AQI pollutants; does not apply.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Evaluate NO<sub>2</sub> (nitrogen dioxide) – explicitly one of the eight standard pollutants used in AQI calculation; applies.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Evaluate N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> (dinitrogen trioxide) – not part of the standard AQI pollutant list; does not apply.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Evaluate N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> (dinitrogen pentoxide) – also not part of the standard AQI pollutant list; does not apply.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Only NO<sub>2</sub> among the four options is an actual CPCB AQI-constituent pollutant.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> Correct option: <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Nitrogen dioxide (NO<sub>2</sub>) is one of the eight pollutants explicitly monitored and used by the CPCB to compute India’s AQI; none of the other nitrogen-oxide variants listed (N<sub>2</sub>O, N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>) are part of this standard set.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (N<sub>2</sub>O)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – A significant greenhouse gas, but not among the CPCB’s AQI pollutant parameters.</li><li><b>Option 2 (NO<sub>2</sub>)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – One of the eight official CPCB AQI pollutants.</li><li><b>Option 3 (N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Not part of the standard AQI pollutant list.</li><li><b>Option 4 (N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub>)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Not part of the standard AQI pollutant list.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>CPCB AQI pollutants: PM<sub>2.5</sub>, PM<sub>10</sub>, <b>NO<sub>2</sub></b>, SO<sub>2</sub>, CO, O<sub>3</sub>, NH<sub>3</sub>, Pb.</li><li>N<sub>2</sub>O, N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O<sub>5</sub> are NOT part of the standard AQI pollutant set.</li><li>Correct answer: NO<sub>2</sub> (Nitrogen dioxide) – <b>Option 2</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_d',no:38,q:'Which among the following are Natural Green House Gases (GHGs)? <ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">A.</span> CO<sub>2</sub> (Carbon dioxide)</li><li><span class="p1-lab">B.</span> H<sub>2</sub>O (Water vapor)</li><li><span class="p1-lab">C.</span> CH<sub>4</sub> (Methane)</li><li><span class="p1-lab">D.</span> O<sub>3</sub> (Ozone)</li><li><span class="p1-lab">E.</span> CFC (Chlorofluorocarbon)</li></ol> Choose the correct answer from the options given below:',opts:['A) A, B, C, D only','B) A, C, D, E only','C) B, C, D, E only','D) A, B, C, E only'],ans:0,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">This tests the distinction between <b>naturally-occurring greenhouse gases</b> (present in Earth’s atmosphere through natural biogeochemical processes long before industrialisation) and <b>synthetic/anthropogenic greenhouse gases</b> (man-made industrial chemicals with no natural source). Carbon dioxide, water vapour, methane and ozone all occur naturally in the atmosphere through natural cycles (respiration, evaporation, wetland/biological methane production, and atmospheric photochemistry respectively). Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), by contrast, are entirely synthetic, human-manufactured compounds with no natural source, developed for industrial/refrigerant use in the 20th century.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Classify each gas as naturally-occurring or purely synthetic/anthropogenic.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> CO<sub>2</sub> (A) – released naturally via respiration, volcanic activity, and the carbon cycle; a natural GHG. Applies.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> H<sub>2</sub>O (B) – water vapour is a naturally-occurring and, in fact, the most abundant natural GHG in the atmosphere. Applies.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> CH<sub>4</sub> (C) – methane is produced naturally by wetlands, termites, and other biological processes (as well as anthropogenic sources); it has a natural origin. Applies.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> O<sub>3</sub> (D) – ozone occurs naturally in the atmosphere (both stratospheric and, at lower concentrations, tropospheric ozone formed via natural photochemical reactions). Applies.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> CFC (E) – chlorofluorocarbons are entirely man-made industrial chemicals with no natural source whatsoever; purely anthropogenic. Does NOT apply.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> The natural GHGs are A, B, C and D only, matching <b>Option 1</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">CO<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O, CH<sub>4</sub> and O<sub>3</sub> all have well-established natural sources and have existed in Earth’s atmosphere long before human industrial activity, whereas CFCs are entirely synthetic compounds engineered by humans (for refrigeration, aerosols, etc.) with no natural analogue – making A, B, C, D the correct set of natural GHGs.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (A, B, C, D only)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – Correctly includes all four naturally-occurring GHGs and excludes the synthetic CFC.</li><li><b>Option 2 (A, C, D, E only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Wrongly includes E (CFC, synthetic) and wrongly excludes B (water vapour, a major natural GHG).</li><li><b>Option 3 (B, C, D, E only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Wrongly includes E (CFC) and excludes A (CO<sub>2</sub>, a fundamental natural GHG).</li><li><b>Option 4 (A, B, C, E only)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Wrongly includes E (CFC) and excludes D (ozone, which does occur naturally).</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Natural GHGs: CO<sub>2</sub>, H<sub>2</sub>O (water vapour), CH<sub>4</sub>, O<sub>3</sub>.</li><li>CFCs are entirely synthetic/anthropogenic – no natural source.</li><li>Correct answer: A, B, C, D only – <b>Option 1</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_d',no:39,q:'Carbon monoxide (CO), an air pollutant when inhaled readily binds with the hemoglobin in the blood stream to form a life threatening compound known as',opts:['A) Hemoglobous','B) Carbo hemoglobin','C) Carbon mono hemoglobin','D) Carboxy hemoglobin'],ans:3,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">This is a direct <b>factual/scientific recall</b> question about the physiological effect of carbon monoxide poisoning. Carbon monoxide binds to haemoglobin far more readily (with much higher affinity) than oxygen does, forming <b>carboxyhemoglobin (COHb)</b> – a stable compound that severely impairs the blood’s ability to transport oxygen to body tissues, making CO exposure potentially fatal even at relatively low concentrations.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Recall the correct scientific/medical terminology for the compound formed when CO binds to haemoglobin.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Eliminate “Hemoglobous” – not a recognised scientific term for any haemoglobin-derived compound.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Eliminate “Carbo hemoglobin” – an incorrect/non-standard variant of the correct term.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Eliminate “Carbon mono hemoglobin” – also a non-standard, incorrectly constructed term.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Confirm “Carboxyhemoglobin” as the correct, standard biochemical/medical term for haemoglobin bound to carbon monoxide.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> This term is universally used in toxicology and clinical medicine to describe CO poisoning’s effect on blood.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> Correct option: <b>Option 4</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">When carbon monoxide is inhaled, it competes with and displaces oxygen at the haemoglobin binding site (with roughly 200–250 times greater affinity than oxygen), forming carboxyhemoglobin – the correct, standard scientific term for this life-threatening compound, which reduces the blood’s oxygen-carrying capacity and can lead to hypoxia and death.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Hemoglobous)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Not a recognised scientific/medical term.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Carbo hemoglobin)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – An incorrectly-formed variant of the actual term.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Carbon mono hemoglobin)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – Also an incorrectly-constructed, non-standard term.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Carboxy hemoglobin)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – The correct, standard scientific term (carboxyhemoglobin) for CO-bound haemoglobin.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>CO binds haemoglobin with far greater affinity than O<sub>2</sub>, forming <b>carboxyhemoglobin</b>.</li><li>This severely reduces the blood’s oxygen-carrying capacity, making CO poisoning potentially fatal.</li><li>Correct answer: Carboxy hemoglobin – <b>Option 4</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'},
  {unit:'p1_u9',paper:'jun23_d',no:40,q:'Which of the following is a chemical treatment in water treatment process?',opts:['A) Filtration','B) Sedimentation','C) Coagulation','D) Screening'],ans:2,exp:'<div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Concept Required</div><div class="p1x-b">Water treatment processes are broadly classified into <b>physical</b> treatments (which rely on mechanical/physical separation, with no chemical reaction involved) and <b>chemical</b> treatments (which rely on adding chemical agents that react with contaminants). <b>Coagulation</b> involves adding chemical coagulants (e.g., alum/aluminium sulphate) that neutralise the electrical charges on suspended particles, causing them to clump together (flocculate) for easier removal – a genuinely chemical process. Filtration, sedimentation and screening, by contrast, are purely physical/mechanical separation processes.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Step-by-Step Solution</div><div class="p1x-b"><ol class="p1-ol"><li><span class="p1-lab">1.</span> <b>Step 1:</b> Classify each listed water-treatment step as physical or chemical.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">2.</span> <b>Step 2:</b> Filtration – passing water through a physical medium (sand, membranes) to mechanically strain out particles; a physical process.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">3.</span> <b>Step 3:</b> Sedimentation – allowing suspended particles to settle out under gravity in a settling tank; a physical process.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">4.</span> <b>Step 4:</b> Coagulation – adding a chemical coagulant that reacts with and destabilises suspended colloidal particles, causing them to aggregate; a chemical process.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">5.</span> <b>Step 5:</b> Screening – passing water through screens/mesh to physically remove large debris; a physical process.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">6.</span> <b>Step 6:</b> Only coagulation among the four options involves the addition of a chemical agent that reacts with contaminants.</li><li><span class="p1-lab">7.</span> <b>Step 7:</b> Correct option: <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ol></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Answer Explanation</div><div class="p1x-b">Coagulation is fundamentally a chemical process: a coagulant chemical (such as alum) is dosed into the water to neutralise particle charges and induce clumping, enabling subsequent physical removal (sedimentation/filtration). Filtration, sedimentation and screening are all purely mechanical/physical separation techniques with no chemical reaction involved, making coagulation the correct answer.</div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Option-wise Analysis</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li><b>Option 1 (Filtration)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – A physical straining process using a filter medium; no chemical reaction occurs.</li><li><b>Option 2 (Sedimentation)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – A physical gravity-settling process; no chemical agent is involved.</li><li><b>Option 3 (Coagulation)</b> <span class="p1-green"><b>Correct</b></span> – Involves the addition of a chemical coagulant that reacts with suspended particles; a genuine chemical treatment step.</li><li><b>Option 4 (Screening)</b> <span class="p1-red"><b>Incorrect</b></span> – A purely physical/mechanical debris-removal process using screens; no chemical reaction involved.</li></ul></div></div><div class="p1x"><div class="p1x-h">Quick revision</div><div class="p1x-b"><ul class="p1-ul"><li>Physical treatment steps: filtration, sedimentation, screening (no chemicals involved).</li><li>Chemical treatment step: coagulation (uses a chemical coagulant, e.g., alum, to aggregate particles).</li><li>Correct answer: Coagulation – <b>Option 3</b>.</li></ul></div></div>'}

);
